var e=JSON.parse('[[3001,"Front-end developers use Docker for:\\n\\n- Running a single development environment (Node.js and the dependencies, for example).\\n\\n- Testing the application in an isolated environment.\\n\\n- Making deployment easier, together with the back end.","Docker lets you package an application and all its dependencies into a container that behaves the same way on any system.\\nAn example of use:\\n\\n- Development:\\n\\n- A `Dockerfile` is created in order to install Node.js and the dependencies:\\n\\n- Running it:\\n\\n- Testing:\\nTests can be run in the container in order to avoid problems with different versions of Node.js.\\n\\n- Deployment:\\nThe front end is built into static files (through `npm run build`, for example), which are then served from an Nginx container:\\n\\nDocker Compose is also used in order to run the front end and the back end together:\\n\\nThis makes teamwork and deployment easier.","FROM node:18\\nWORKDIR /app\\nCOPY package.json .\\nRUN npm install\\nCOPY . .\\nCMD [\\"npm\\", \\"run\\", \\"dev\\"]\\n\\ndocker build -t frontend-app .\\ndocker run -p 3000:3000 frontend-app\\n\\nFROM nginx:alpine\\nCOPY dist /usr/share/nginx/html\\n\\nservices:\\n  frontend:\\n    build: ./frontend\\n    ports: [\\"3000:3000\\"]\\n  backend:\\n    build: ./backend\\n    ports: [\\"5000:5000\\"]"],[3002,"Docker is a platform for creating, packaging and running containers, which isolate an application and its dependencies.","1. Containers use the host system\'s kernel but are isolated through namespaces and cgroups.\\n2. The main components:\\n\\n- Dockerfile — the description of an image\\n\\n- Image — a ready-made packaged environment\\n\\n- Container — a running instance of an image\\n\\n- Docker Hub — a repository of images\\n\\n3. It makes deployment, testing and scaling easier.",null],[3003,"Docker uses the host\'s kernel and does not emulate an operating system, unlike virtual machines.","Docker:\\n\\n- A shared host kernel\\n\\n- Fast start-up (seconds)\\n\\n- Light weight (~MB)\\n\\n- Isolation at the process level\\n\\nA virtual machine:\\n\\n- Its own operating system + kernel\\n\\n- Slow start-up (minutes)\\n\\n- A large footprint (GB)\\n\\n- Isolation at the hypervisor level\\n\\nWhen to use which:\\n\\n- Docker — for microservices and fast CI/CD\\n\\n- A VM — when you need a full operating system and strong isolation",null],[3004,"Each microservice should have its own `Dockerfile`, minimal dependencies and an independent image. Version tags, environment variable configuration and CI/CD for building and delivering automatically are used.","1. Separating the services:\\n\\n- Each microservice is a separate repository or folder.\\n\\n- Its own `Dockerfile` with a minimal environment (`python:slim`, `alpine`).\\n\\n2. Using versioning:\\n\\n- Image tags should include version numbers (`v1.0.0`, `latest`).\\n\\n3. Managing dependencies:\\n\\n- Use virtual environments or `requirements.txt`.\\n\\n- Packages should be \\"frozen\\" (through `pip freeze`).\\n\\n4. Secrets and configurations:\\n\\n- Do not keep them in the image; use environment variables and a Kubernetes ConfigMap/Secret.\\n\\n5. Integration with CI/CD:\\n\\n- Images are built automatically after a commit.\\n\\n- Delivery to a Docker registry and deployment to the cluster\\n\\nWhen to use it:\\nContainerisation is necessary for scalable, independent microservices, especially in Kubernetes and cloud infrastructure.",null],[3005,"The typical problems are large images, resource leaks, mistakes in the Dockerfile, caching problems, port conflicts, volumes not working correctly and dependence on the environment. Difficulties with security are also fairly common.","1. A large image size:\\n\\n- Often because unnecessary packages are installed.\\n\\n- The solution is to use `alpine` and multi-stage builds.\\n\\n2. Resource leaks and zombie processes:\\n\\n- Containers shutting down incorrectly.\\n\\n- `CMD` and `ENTRYPOINT` should be used correctly.\\n\\n3. Problems with layer caching:\\n\\n- Changing the upper layers leads to a complete rebuild.\\n\\n- The instructions in the Dockerfile need to be ordered properly.\\n\\n4. Errors when mounting volumes:\\n\\n- A lack of synchronisation, differences in paths between the host and the container.\\n\\n5. Port conflicts:\\n\\n- The port is already taken on the host. You have to use `docker ps` and `netstat`.\\n\\n6. Security:\\n\\n- Do not use root inside the container.\\n\\n- Keep images updated and do not use `latest` unless it is necessary.",null],[3006,"Docker implements process isolation, portability of the environment, repeatable builds, minimalism and a declarative description of the infrastructure. Containers are created from images that can be run on any machine with Docker.","The principles:\\n\\n- Isolation:\\nEach container works independently, as a separate process in its own namespace and with its own file system.\\n\\n- Reproducibility:\\nEvery dependency and the environment are described in the `Dockerfile`. The container will behave the same way on any host.\\n\\n- Continuity:\\nContainers can be built and deployed automatically in CI/CD.\\n\\n- Modularity:\\nSplitting into microservices and using several containers in one project (through `docker-compose`, for example).\\n\\n- Light weight:\\nContainers use a single operating system kernel and start faster than virtual machines.\\n\\n- Portability:\\nRunning the same containers in different environments: locally, in the cloud, on a server.",null],[3007,"A Java application can be containerised by creating a Dockerfile that states a base image with the JDK or JRE, copies the jar file in and declares the start-up command. That lets the application run in isolation, independently of the environment\'s settings. The main steps are building the jar file, writing the Dockerfile and building the image with the `docker build` command.","Containerisation is a way of packaging an application together with all its dependencies into a single portable container. For Java applications the process is as follows:\\n1. Building the jar file\\nUsually with `Maven` or `Gradle`:\\n\\nThis will create the file `target/app.jar`.\\n2. Creating a Dockerfile\\nA simple `Dockerfile` might look like this:\\n\\n- `FROM` — the base image with Java.\\n\\n- `COPY` — copies the jar file inside the container.\\n\\n- `ENTRYPOINT` — states the start-up command.\\n\\n3. Building the Docker image\\n\\n4. Running the container\\n\\n- The container is now available on port 8080.\\n\\n- You can use environment variables, volumes and other Docker options when starting it.\\n\\nAlternatives\\n\\n- Jib from Google — it lets you build images without a `Dockerfile`, straight from Maven/Gradle.\\n\\n- Multi-stage builds — optimising the image\'s size by separating the build and runtime stages.\\n\\nWhen to use it:\\n\\n- When the application needs to behave identically in every environment (dev/test/prod).\\n\\n- When you want to deploy to Kubernetes, AWS ECS, GCP Cloud Run and other orchestrators.","mvn clean package\\n\\nFROM openjdk:17-jdk-slim\\nCOPY target/app.jar app.jar\\nENTRYPOINT [\\"java\\", \\"-jar\\", \\"app.jar\\"]\\n\\ndocker build -t my-java-app .\\n\\ndocker run -p 8080:8080 my-java-app"],[3008,"Docker is often used for containerisation, letting you isolate environments and spin test stands up quickly.\\nMessage brokers such as Kafka or RabbitMQ are used to test queues and asynchronous interactions.\\nFor load testing, JMeter, Gatling and Locust are popular, letting you model a large number of users and check how robust the system is.","Containerisation\\n\\n- Docker — the basis for isolating services and deploying a test environment, especially in a microservice architecture.\\n\\n- QA specialists use Docker for:\\n\\n- bringing a test environment up quickly (through `docker-compose`, for example),\\n\\n- simulating external services,\\n\\n- reproducing bugs in an identical environment.\\n\\n- Kubernetes is sometimes used for scalable management of containers, especially in load testing.\\n\\nMessage brokers:\\n\\n- Kafka — a distributed system for passing messages between services. It lets you test:\\n\\n- the correctness of producers/consumers,\\n\\n- the order and integrity of messages,\\n\\n- delivery latency.\\n\\n- RabbitMQ — a broker with support for queues, routing and retries. It is convenient for UI and API testing of systems with deferred processing.\\n\\n- It is important to inspect messages using utilities (`kafkacat`, the `RabbitMQ Management UI`) and the logs.\\n\\nLoad testing\\n\\n- JMeter — the classic tool for creating scripts that simulate a large number of users and operations. It supports HTTP, JDBC, SOAP and Kafka.\\n\\n- Gatling — a tool written in Scala, suited to scenarios with a high degree of customisation.\\n\\n- Locust — a Python tool, which gives flexibility because the scenarios are written as code.\\n\\n- They are used to check:\\n\\n- the performance of an API and a database,\\n\\n- robustness under load,\\n\\n- the limits and memory leaks.",null],[3009,"The main problem is the different architectures (ARM/x86). The solution: use multi-architecture images (`--platform linux/amd64`).","The problems:\\n\\n- ARM servers do not run x86 images.\\n\\n- Different versions of glibc can cause errors.\\n\\nThe solution:\\n\\nConclusion:\\nAlways state the platform when building.","docker build --platform linux/amd64 -t my-image ."],[3010,"Horizontal scaling is achieved through:\\n\\n- Load balancing (Nginx, HAProxy).\\n\\n- Containerisation (Docker + Kubernetes).\\n\\n- Distributed databases (Postgres replication, Redis Cluster).","1. Load balancing:\\n\\n- Nginx distributes requests between servers.\\n\\n- HAProxy — for more complex scenarios (WebSocket, for example).\\n\\n2. Containerisation:\\n\\n- Docker for running services in isolation.\\n\\n- Kubernetes for automatic scaling (HPA).\\n\\n3. Databases:\\n\\n- PostgreSQL: setting up read replicas.\\n\\n- Redis Cluster for a distributed cache.\\n\\nAn example Kubernetes HPA:\\n\\nConclusion:\\nHorizontal scaling requires a load balancer, an orchestrator and distributed databases.","apiVersion: autoscaling/v2\\nkind: HorizontalPodAutoscaler\\nmetadata:\\n  name: my-app\\nspec:\\n  scaleTargetRef:\\n    apiVersion: apps/v1\\n    kind: Deployment\\n    name: my-app\\n  minReplicas: 2\\n  maxReplicas: 10\\n  metrics:\\n    - type: Resource\\n      resource:\\n        name: cpu\\n        target:\\n          type: Utilization\\n          averageUtilization: 80"],[3011,"A Deployment manages stateless applications (the replicas are identical). A StatefulSet is used for stateful services — the pods have fixed names and volumes bound to them.","Deployment:\\n\\n- it manages stateless applications;\\n\\n- identical pods without identifiers;\\n\\n- updates are a rolling update.\\n\\nStatefulSet:\\n\\n- fixed pods (pod-0, pod-1);\\n\\n- a persistent volume for each pod;\\n\\n- the order of starting and stopping matters.\\n\\nAn example of use:\\n\\n- Deployment → an API service;\\n\\n- StatefulSet → a PostgreSQL cluster.\\n\\nConclusion: use a StatefulSet only for services that hold state.",null],[3012,"In PyCharm you need to go to Settings → Project → Python Interpreter → Add Interpreter → On Docker. Choose the container image or use Docker Compose. The IDE will connect to the interpreter in the container automatically for running and debugging code.","Setting up a remote interpreter lets you develop in an environment identical to production.\\nThe steps for adding one:\\n\\n- Open Settings (Ctrl+Alt+S).\\n\\n- Go to Project → Python Interpreter.\\n\\n- Click the gear icon → Add.\\n\\n- Choose \\"Docker\\" or \\"Docker Compose\\".\\n\\n- Give the container image or the docker-compose.yml.\\n\\n- Choose the path to the interpreter inside the container (usually /usr/bin/python3).\\n\\nThe advantages:\\n\\n- A single environment for development and production.\\n\\n- Dependencies are easy to manage.",null],[3013,"A Deployment manages stateless applications, providing scaling and updates. A StatefulSet is needed for stateful applications, where stable names and storage matter.","Deployment:\\n\\n- it suits microservices without state;\\n\\n- the replicas are identical;\\n\\n- it supports rolling updates.\\n\\nStatefulSet:\\n\\n- it gives fixed DNS names;\\n\\n- it keeps data on a PersistentVolume;\\n\\n- it suits databases and clusters.\\n\\nConclusion: a Deployment is for stateless APIs. A StatefulSet is for stateful services (Postgres, Kafka, Redis Cluster).",null],[3014,"A Liveness Probe checks whether the container is alive. A Readiness Probe checks whether it is ready to serve traffic. If readiness does not pass, no traffic will be routed to it.","Liveness Probe: if it fails, the container is restarted. It is used to recover hung processes.\\nReadiness Probe: if it fails, the container stays alive but is taken out of the load balancer. It is used for a graceful start and for updates.\\n\\nConclusion: configuring the probes correctly provides robustness and smooth releases.",null],[3015,"A Deployment manages an application\'s replicas, scaled horizontally. A DaemonSet guarantees that there is one pod on every node of the cluster (for a logger or a monitoring agent, for example).","Deployment:\\n\\n- It manages the number of replicas.\\n\\n- It suits stateless applications (an API, a front end).\\n\\n- It allows a rolling update and a rollback.\\n\\nDaemonSet:\\n\\n- It deploys a pod on every node of the cluster.\\n\\n- It is used for infrastructure services:\\n\\n- log agents (fluentd, filebeat),\\n\\n- monitoring (node-exporter).\\n\\nExample:\\n\\nConclusion: a Deployment = scalable applications. A DaemonSet = one pod per node (agents, daemons).","apiVersion: apps/v1\\nkind: DaemonSet\\nmetadata: { name: node-exporter }\\nspec:\\n  selector: { matchLabels: { app: node-exporter } }\\n  template:\\n    metadata: { labels: { app: node-exporter } }\\n    spec:\\n      containers:\\n      - name: node-exporter\\n        image: prom/node-exporter"],[3016,"The main methods are: using a multi-stage build, choosing a minimal base image (`alpine`, for example), combining `RUN` commands into one in order to reduce the number of layers, using a `.dockerignore` file and clearing the package manager\'s cache in the same layer where it was used.","Large images take up a lot of space in the registry and take longer to download and deploy. Optimising the image is an important part of the CI/CD pipeline.\\nThe key methods of optimisation:\\n\\n- A multi-stage build:\\n\\n- This is the most effective method for applications that have to be compiled.\\n\\n- It lets you use one image (with the full SDK) for building and compiling and then copy only the finished artefacts (a `dll` or an executable, for example) into a small final image (with the runtime only).\\n\\n- Using minimal base images:\\n\\n- Instead of full images such as `ubuntu`, use minimal ones: `alpine`, `distroless`, or the official `-slim`/`-alpine` versions.\\n\\n- Example: `mcr.microsoft.com/dotnet/aspnet:8.0-alpine` is considerably smaller than `mcr.microsoft.com/dotnet/aspnet:8.0`.\\n\\n- Minimising the number of layers:\\n\\n- Each instruction in a Dockerfile (`RUN`, `COPY`, `ADD`) creates a new layer.\\n\\n- Combine related `RUN` commands using `&&` and `\\\\` for line continuations.\\n\\n- Using .dockerignore:\\n\\n- It works like `.gitignore`. It excludes unnecessary files from the build context (`.git`, `bin`, `obj`, `node_modules`, IDE configuration files), which could inflate the image for no reason.\\n\\n- Clearing the cache in the same layer:\\n\\n- Remove package managers\' caches (apt, npm, nuget) in the same `RUN` instruction in which you installed those packages. That prevents the cache being kept in the final image.\\n\\nConclusion:\\nThe combination of a multi-stage build and a minimal base image gives the biggest gain in size. The other methods are best practices for keeping the image clean and optimising it further.","# The build stage\\nFROM mcr.microsoft.com/dotnet/sdk:8.0 AS build\\nWORKDIR /src\\nCOPY . .\\nRUN dotnet publish -c Release -o /app\\n\\n# The final stage\\nFROM mcr.microsoft.com/dotnet/aspnet:8.0-alpine AS final\\nWORKDIR /app\\nCOPY --from=build /app .\\nENTRYPOINT [\\"dotnet\\", \\"MyApp.dll\\"]\\n\\n# BAD: creates 3 layers.\\nRUN apt-get update\\nRUN apt-get install -y python3\\nRUN rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*\\n\\n# GOOD: creates 1 layer.\\nRUN apt-get update && \\\\\\n    apt-get install -y python3 && \\\\\\n    rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*"],[3017,"After the `docker build` command, which uses the instructions from the Dockerfile, has run successfully, we get a Docker image. That image is an immutable template holding a file system (with every dependency, the application\'s code and the settings) and metadata describing how to start a container from this image. The image itself is not a running application but rather a \\"class\\" or a \\"blueprint\\" for creating containers.","A Dockerfile is a text file with instructions for building an image. The build is the sequential execution of those instructions.\\nThe build process and its result:\\n\\n- The build context: the Docker client sends the folder containing the Dockerfile and all the files in it (the context) to the Docker daemon.\\n\\n- Reading the instructions: the daemon reads the Dockerfile line by line, executing each instruction.\\n\\n- Creating layers: each instruction (`RUN`, `COPY`, `ADD` and so on) creates a new layer in the image\'s file system.\\n\\n- Intermediate containers: some instructions start temporary containers in order to run commands (`RUN apt-get update`, for example).\\n\\n- Forming the image: once every instruction has run, the final, immutable Docker image is formed.\\n\\nWhat exactly does the resulting image contain?\\n\\n- A set of read-only layers: each layer is the result of one instruction from the Dockerfile.\\n\\n- A file system: the union of all those layers forms a complete file system (Ubuntu + PHP + Nginx + your application\'s code, for example).\\n\\n- Metadata:\\n\\n- Environment variables (set through `ENV`).\\n\\n- The default command to run (given through `CMD` or `ENTRYPOINT`).\\n\\n- Exposed ports (declared through `EXPOSE`).\\n\\n- Volumes (VOLUME) and other configuration.\\n\\nConclusion: a Dockerfile is the recipe, and a Docker image is the finished \\"dish\\", packaged for further use. That image can be kept in a registry (such as Docker Hub), passed to other developers and to servers, and used to start many identical containers. A container itself is a running instance of that image.",null],[3018,"A Docker image\'s size is reduced by using lightweight base images, multi-stage builds, clearing caches and packages, copying only the files that are needed and pinning dependencies.\\nMulti-stage builds let you separate the build stages from the runtime stage, leaving only a minimal set of files in the final image.\\nSmall images speed delivery up, shorten deployment time and improve security.","Docker images can grow in size quickly, especially when they contain many development tools or temporary files. To speed up delivery of the application and reduce the load on CI/CD, it is important to optimise the final image size.\\nWays of reducing Docker images\\n1. Using minimal base images\\nInstead of:\\n\\nthey use:\\n\\nor even:\\n\\nAlpine is the lightest image, but it may need more configuration.\\n2. Multi-stage build\\nIt lets you separate building the application from the final runtime.\\nExample:\\ndockerfile\\n\\nThe advantages:\\n\\n- dev dependencies are removed\\n\\n- the final image contains only the application and the runtime\\n\\n3. Clearing the cache and temporary files\\nWhen installing system packages:\\ndockerfile\\n\\n4. Copying only the files that are needed\\nUse `.dockerignore`:\\ntext\\n\\n5. Minimising the number of layers\\nEach `RUN`, `COPY` and `ADD` instruction creates a new layer.\\nCombine them:\\n\\n6. Using Poetry or pip + constraints.txt\\nPinning dependencies reduces the number of rebuilds.\\nAdditional strategies\\n\\n- packaging the application as a wheel\\n\\n- static binary builds (Go/Rust)\\n\\n- an alpine-based runtime + a builder stage\\n\\nConclusion\\nOptimising Docker images makes deployment faster, CI/CD more stable and the infrastructure cheaper. The best practices are multi-stage builds, minimal images, clearing the cache and organising files sensibly.","FROM python:3.11\\n\\nFROM python:3.11-slim\\n\\nFROM python:3.11-alpine\\n\\nFROM python:3.11 as builder\\nWORKDIR /app\\nCOPY requirements.txt .\\nRUN pip install --prefix=/install -r requirements.txt\\n\\nFROM python:3.11-slim\\nCOPY --from=builder /install /usr/local\\nCOPY . /app\\nCMD [\\"python\\", \\"main.py\\"]\\n\\nRUN apt-get update && apt-get install -y gcc \\\\\\n    && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*\\n\\n__pycache__\\n.git\\ntests\\nnotebooks\\n\\nRUN pip install -r req.txt && python setup.py install"],[3019,"Prometheus is a monitoring system that collects metrics from services over HTTP, stores them as time series and lets you run powerful queries through the PromQL language.\\nIt works on a pull model: it polls `/metrics` endpoints regularly itself rather than waiting for applications to send data.\\nPrometheus integrates with Kubernetes, supports alerts through Alertmanager and is easy to visualise in Grafana.\\nThat makes it the standard for monitoring microservice and cloud systems.","Prometheus is an open monitoring and alerting system oriented towards collecting time-series metrics. It is widely used in modern distributed systems because of its reliability, flexibility and deep integration with Kubernetes.\\nPrometheus\'s main features\\n1. The pull model (polling)\\nPrometheus\'s core idea is that it polls services regularly itself:\\n\\n- every N seconds it requests `/metrics`\\n\\n- it receives the metrics in a text format\\n\\n- it stores them as time series\\n\\nThat approach has upsides:\\n\\n- simple scaling\\n\\n- no need for centralised sending\\n\\n- easy discovery of new services (through Kubernetes Service Discovery, for example)\\n\\n2. The metrics format\\nMetrics look like this:\\ntext\\n\\nThe structure:\\n\\n- the metric\'s name\\n\\n- a set of labels\\n\\n- the value\\n\\nThat lets data be filtered and aggregated flexibly.\\n3. PromQL — the query language\\nPrometheus includes its own query language, which lets you:\\n\\n- compute rates of change (`rate()`)\\n\\n- aggregate data (`sum()`, `avg()`, `max()`)\\n\\n- build complex alerting rules\\n\\nExample:\\ntext\\n\\nWe get \\"requests per second\\".\\n4. Integration with Kubernetes\\nKubernetes can add services as targets automatically, which makes Prometheus the de facto standard for monitoring containers.\\nThe objects used are:\\n\\n- ServiceMonitor\\n\\n- PodMonitor\\n\\n5. Alertmanager\\nA separate component of the system, which:\\n\\n- receives the alerting rules\\n\\n- sends notifications (Slack, email, PagerDuty)\\n\\n- supports grouping and suppressing alerts\\n\\nAn example rule:\\nyaml\\n\\n6. The ecosystem of exporters\\nPrometheus can monitor anything, even systems with no support for metrics:\\n\\n- node_exporter — server metrics\\n\\n- postgres_exporter — PostgreSQL\\n\\n- redis_exporter — Redis\\n\\n- blackbox_exporter — the availability of services\\n\\nThat makes the system universal.\\n7. The difference from other systems (ELK or Zabbix, for example)\\nPrometheus focuses on metrics, not logs.\\nIt is optimised for high-frequency time series and works excellently in environments with dynamic services.\\nConclusion\\nPrometheus is a powerful, flexible and scalable monitoring system built around the ideas of:\\n\\n- a pull model for collecting metrics\\n\\n- time series\\n\\n- the PromQL language\\n\\n- automatic discovery of services (especially in Kubernetes)\\n\\n- simple, reliable alerting\\n\\nThat makes it the main tool for monitoring microservices and cloud-native infrastructure.","http_requests_total{method=\\"GET\\", status=\\"200\\"} 1523\\napp_active_users 24\\n\\nrate(http_requests_total[5m])\\n\\n- alert: HighErrorRate\\n  expr: rate(http_requests_total{status=\\"500\\"}[5m]) > 1\\n  for: 2m\\n  labels:\\n    severity: warning\\n  annotations:\\n    description: \\"Error rate is high\\""],[3020,"Efficient memory use is achieved by reducing the number of allocations, using pools, keeping leaks under control, optimising data structures and monitoring. Memory profiling tools, releasing resources correctly and careful handling of caching all matter. At the environment level, configuring container limits and using the operating system\'s facilities help.","What affects memory in high-load systems\\nMemory is one of a server\'s key resources, and using it inefficiently leads to slowdowns, out-of-memory conditions and degraded operation.\\nThe main methods of optimising memory\\n\\n- Reducing allocations and the number of objects\\n\\n- Using `slots`\\n\\n- Switching to arrays or numpy\\n\\n- Minimising temporary objects\\n\\n- Static and pre-allocated structures\\n\\n- Connection pools\\n\\n- Fixed-size buffers\\n\\n- Pre-creating objects\\n\\n- Optimising caching\\n\\n- Limiting the cache\'s size\\n\\n- Using a TTL\\n\\n- Applying LRU mechanisms\\n\\n- Memory profiling\\n\\n- `tracemalloc`\\n\\n- `memory_profiler`\\n\\n- Looking for leaks and objects that are too large\\n\\n- Handling threads and processes correctly\\n\\n- Avoiding copying large structures\\n\\n- Using shared memory\\n\\n- Optimisation in containers and the operating system\\n\\n- Controlling `cgroups`\\n\\n- Docker\'s memory limits\\n\\n- Monitoring through Prometheus/Grafana\\n\\nAn example of memory profiling\\n\\nConclusion\\nEfficient memory use is a combination of optimising data structures, reducing allocations, profiling and configuring the environment sensibly.","import tracemalloc\\n\\ntracemalloc.start()\\n# ... your code ...\\nprint(tracemalloc.get_traced_memory())"],[3021,"`docker stop` tries to stop the container gracefully, giving the application time to finish. `docker kill` terminates the container immediately, without waiting. In the first case the application receives a signal so that it can shut down cleanly. In the second the process is simply killed by force. `docker stop` is usually preferable in production.","Both commands are used to stop containers, but they do it in fundamentally different ways, which matters for applications\' stability.\\nDefinition\\nContainer termination is the process of shutting a container and every process inside it down.\\n1. docker stop\\nThe `docker stop` command works \\"gently\\".\\n1.1. How it works\\nThe sequence of actions:\\n\\n- Docker sends the container\'s main process the `SIGTERM` signal.\\n\\n- Docker waits for a set time (`--time`, ~10 seconds by default).\\n\\n- If the process has not finished, Docker sends `SIGKILL`.\\n\\n1.2. What that gives you\\nThe application can:\\n\\n- close connections;\\n\\n- save its state;\\n\\n- finish background tasks properly.\\n\\nThis is the standard and recommended way of stopping a container.\\n2. docker kill\\nThe `docker kill` command terminates the container immediately.\\n2.1. How it works\\n\\n- Docker sends the `SIGKILL` signal straight away (or another one, if it is given explicitly).\\n\\n- The process cannot intercept or handle that signal.\\n\\n2.2. The consequences\\n\\n- data may not be saved;\\n\\n- files may be corrupted;\\n\\n- external systems do not get sessions closed properly.\\n\\n3. When to use which\\n\\n- `docker stop` — almost always in production;\\n\\n- `docker kill` — for emergencies or hung containers.\\n\\n4. In short\\n`docker stop` is the safe, correct way of stopping a container, while `docker kill` is a last resort for terminating one immediately. In normal operation `docker stop` is always preferred.",null],[3022,"When `docker stop` runs, the `SIGTERM` signal is sent to the container. That signal tells the process it is being asked to shut down gracefully. The process can intercept the signal and clean its resources up. If the container does not finish in time, Docker will send `SIGKILL`. That way a balance is struck between doing it properly and terminating reliably.","Docker uses standard Unix signals to manage the processes inside a container.\\nDefinition\\nSIGTERM is an operating system signal that asks a process to shut down gracefully.\\n1. SIGTERM\'s behaviour\\nWhen a process receives `SIGTERM`, it can:\\n\\n- intercept the signal;\\n\\n- run cleanup logic;\\n\\n- exit by itself.\\n\\nThat gives the application a chance to:\\n\\n- close connections;\\n\\n- finish writing data;\\n\\n- release resources.\\n\\n2. Docker\'s role on docker stop\\nWhen the command runs:\\n\\n- Docker sends `SIGTERM` to the process with PID 1 in the container;\\n\\n- a timer waiting for it to finish starts.\\n\\nThe parameter:\\n\\n- `--time` lets you set the waiting time by hand.\\n\\n3. What happens next\\nIf the process:\\n\\n- finished gracefully → the container is stopped;\\n\\n- did not finish → Docker sends `SIGKILL`.\\n\\n4. Practical recommendations\\n\\n- handle `SIGTERM` in server applications;\\n\\n- shut background threads down properly;\\n\\n- do not ignore the signal.\\n\\n5. In short\\n`docker stop` sends `SIGTERM` first, giving the application the chance to shut down gracefully. This is the key mechanism for stopping containers safely.",null],[3023,"If the container has not finished after receiving `SIGTERM`, Docker sends it `SIGKILL`. That signal terminates the process by force, with no chance of handling it. The container will be stopped in any case. Such a termination can lead to data loss. So applications should respond to `SIGTERM` properly.","Docker always guarantees that the container will be stopped, even if the application inside it has hung or ignores signals.\\nDefinition\\nSIGKILL is a signal that terminates a process immediately, with no chance of intercepting it.\\n1. The waiting timeout\\nAfter sending `SIGTERM`, Docker:\\n\\n- waits for a set time (`--time`);\\n\\n- does not check \\"what the process is doing\\" inside.\\n\\nIf the timeout expires, a graceful shutdown is considered impossible.\\n2. Forced termination\\nDocker sends `SIGKILL`:\\n\\n- the process finishes instantly;\\n\\n- the cleanup code does not run;\\n\\n- the data in memory is lost.\\n\\n3. The possible consequences\\nForced termination can lead to:\\n\\n- lost data;\\n\\n- an inconsistent state;\\n\\n- broken connections.\\n\\nIt is especially dangerous for:\\n\\n- databases;\\n\\n- file operations;\\n\\n- stateful services.\\n\\n4. How to avoid it\\nThe recommended practices:\\n\\n- handle `SIGTERM` quickly;\\n\\n- do not perform long blocking operations during shutdown;\\n\\n- configure `--time` properly.\\n\\n5. In short\\nIf the container does not respond to `SIGTERM`, Docker uses `SIGKILL` as a last resort. That guarantees it stops, but it can be dangerous for data, so applications should be able to shut down gracefully.",null],[3024,"`docker kill` is justified when a container has hung and does not respond to `docker stop`. It is used if the process ignores `SIGTERM` or is stuck in a broken state. The command can also be useful when testing how robust a system is against sudden failures. In ordinary working scenarios it is best avoided. It is a last resort, not a standard management tool.","`docker kill` is a tool for forced termination, applied when a graceful shutdown is impossible or pointless.\\nDefinition\\ndocker kill is a Docker command that sends the container a termination signal immediately (`SIGKILL` by default).\\n1. A hung or broken process\\nThe main use case:\\n\\n- the container does not respond to `SIGTERM`;\\n\\n- `docker stop` has no effect;\\n\\n- the process is in a deadlock or waiting forever.\\n\\nIn such a situation there is no point waiting for a graceful shutdown.\\n2. Emergencies\\n`docker kill` is used when:\\n\\n- the container consumes 100 % of the CPU or the memory;\\n\\n- it puts excessive load on the system;\\n\\n- it interferes with other services.\\n\\nThe goal is to free resources quickly.\\n3. Robustness testing\\nSometimes `docker kill` is used deliberately:\\n\\n- for chaos testing;\\n\\n- to check how the system behaves when a service fails suddenly;\\n\\n- to test retry logic and fault tolerance.\\n\\nThis is done in a controlled environment.\\n4. When you should not use it\\n`docker kill` is not appropriate if:\\n\\n- the container can be stopped gracefully;\\n\\n- the service works with important data;\\n\\n- the state needs to be preserved.\\n\\nIn those cases `docker stop` is preferable.\\n5. In short\\n`docker kill` is justified in emergency and test scenarios, when a graceful shutdown is impossible or unnecessary. In normal operation it is a last resort.",null],[3025,"The question tests your understanding of the consequences of shutting containers down improperly and the effect on data and on the system as a whole.","Forced termination is always a risk, especially for stateful applications and systems with side effects.\\nDefinition\\nForced termination risk means the negative consequences that arise when a process is terminated immediately without a graceful shutdown.\\n1. Data loss\\nWith `SIGKILL`:\\n\\n- the data in memory is not saved;\\n\\n- buffers are not flushed to disk;\\n\\n- unfinished operations are lost.\\n\\nThat is critical for:\\n\\n- databases;\\n\\n- write-back caches;\\n\\n- file operations.\\n\\n2. A broken state\\nForced termination can leave:\\n\\n- partially written files;\\n\\n- uncommitted transactions;\\n\\n- services out of sync with each other.\\n\\nRecovering from such a failure is harder.\\n3. Broken network connections\\nThe container:\\n\\n- does not close connections properly;\\n\\n- does not send final responses;\\n\\n- may cause timeouts for clients.\\n\\nThat is especially dangerous with synchronous API calls.\\n4. Cascading failures\\nIn distributed systems:\\n\\n- one service failing can trigger retries;\\n\\n- the load on the other components grows;\\n\\n- a \\"domino\\" effect is possible.\\n\\n5. Difficult diagnosis\\nAfter `docker kill`:\\n\\n- the logs may be incomplete;\\n\\n- it is hard to tell at which stage the failure happened;\\n\\n- reproducing the problem is difficult.\\n\\n6. In short\\nTerminating a container by force is dangerous because of data loss, a broken state and cascading failures. It should be used only in exceptional cases.",null],[3026,"Docker is used to package an application together with its environment. That lets the code run identically on any server. Docker makes development, testing and deployment easier. It helps avoid problems with dependencies. In back-end development Docker has become the standard.","Docker solves one of the main problems of back-end development — the differences between the development, testing and production environments.\\nDefinition\\nDocker is a containerisation tool that lets an application run together with all its dependencies in an isolated environment.\\nDocker\'s main jobs on the back end\\nDocker is used not for Docker\'s own sake but for predictability and repeatability.\\n1. A single environment\\nDocker guarantees that:\\n\\n- the version of Python is the same\\n\\n- the dependencies match\\n\\n- the system libraries are identical\\n\\nThe phrase \\"it works on my machine\\" stops being a problem.\\n2. Easier local development\\nWith Docker:\\n\\n- you do not need to install a database locally\\n\\n- you do not need to set the environment up by hand\\n\\n- the project comes up with a single command\\n\\n3. Isolation of services\\nEach service:\\n\\n- runs in its own container\\n\\n- does not conflict with the others\\n\\n- has clear boundaries of responsibility\\n\\nThat matters especially for microservices.\\n4. Easier CI/CD\\nIn CI, Docker:\\n\\n- is used to build images\\n\\n- to run tests in an isolated environment\\n\\n- to deploy to servers and to Kubernetes\\n\\n5. Scalability and portability\\nA Docker container:\\n\\n- behaves the same way locally and in the cloud\\n\\n- scales easily\\n\\n- suits orchestration\\n\\nConclusion\\nDocker is a basic tool of the back-end developer, which makes applications portable, reproducible and convenient for teamwork and automation.","docker-compose up"],[3027,"Kubernetes is an open-source platform for the orchestration of containers. It automates the deployment, the management and the scaling of containerized applications. Instead of managing containers by hand, Kubernetes lets you use declarative configurations in order to set them up. That helps you run applications reliably, ensuring their fault tolerance and flexibility.","Kubernetes (often abbreviated as K8s) is a system for the automatic management of containerized applications. It solves the problems of manual deployment, updating and scaling, providing powerful mechanisms of automation.\\nKubernetes\'s main capabilities:\\n\\n- The orchestration of containers - it manages several containers on different servers.\\n\\n- Automatic scaling - it increases or reduces the number of running containers depending on the load.\\n\\n- Self-healing - it restarts the containers in the event of failures.\\n\\n- The balancing of the load - it distributes the traffic between the containers evenly.\\n\\n- Declarative management - the users describe the system\'s desired state, and Kubernetes maintains it automatically.\\n\\nAn example of the use:\\nSuppose you have a web application working in a Docker container. Instead of starting the containers on the servers by hand, you can create a Kubernetes manifest:\\n\\nThat file states that there have to be three working instances of the `my-app` container. Kubernetes will itself deploy them, distribute them between the servers and ensure their stable work.\\nKubernetes is needed for the automated management of container applications. It makes the deployment and the maintenance of systems simpler, more reliable and more flexible.","apiVersion: apps/v1\\nkind: Deployment\\nmetadata:\\n  name: my-app\\nspec:\\n  replicas: 3\\n  selector:\\n    matchLabels:\\n      app: my-app\\n  template:\\n    metadata:\\n      labels:\\n        app: my-app\\n    spec:\\n      containers:\\n      - name: my-app\\n        image: my-app:latest"],[3028,"Kubernetes automates the management of containers, making the deployment, the scaling and the balancing of the load easier. It makes applications fault-tolerant and lets the resources be distributed evenly. Thanks to that, complex systems with hundreds of containers can be managed easily.","The use of Kubernetes gives a multitude of advantages, especially when working with microservices and cloud applications.\\nThe main advantages:\\n\\n- Automation - Kubernetes itself manages the containers, restarting them when they fail.\\n\\n- The flexibility of the scaling - it adds or removes containers depending on the load.\\n\\n- The balancing of the load - it distributes the requests between the containers evenly.\\n\\n- Updates without downtime - new versions of an application can be deployed without stopping the service.\\n\\n- The effective use of the resources - the containers run on the cluster\'s different nodes, saving capacity.\\n\\nAn example:\\nIf you have a web application, Kubernetes can increase the number of instances automatically during high traffic and reduce it when the traffic is low.\\n\\nThat manifest states that Kubernetes scales the application automatically when the CPU load exceeds 50%.\\nKubernetes makes the management of containers simple and reliable. It ensures automatic scaling and fault tolerance and makes the updates easier.","apiVersion: autoscaling/v2\\nkind: HorizontalPodAutoscaler\\nmetadata:\\n  name: my-app-hpa\\nspec:\\n  scaleTargetRef:\\n    apiVersion: apps/v1\\n    kind: Deployment\\n    name: my-app\\n  minReplicas: 2\\n  maxReplicas: 10\\n  metrics:\\n  - type: Resource\\n    resource:\\n      name: cpu\\n      target:\\n        type: Utilization\\n        averageUtilization: 50"],[3029,"A Kubernetes cluster is a group of nodes (servers) on which containerized applications are run. In a cluster there is a managing component (the control plane) and worker nodes, which run the containers. Kubernetes manages the cluster, ensuring the automatic deployment, scaling and balancing of the load.","A Kubernetes cluster is the main unit of infrastructure in Kubernetes. It unites several servers, ensuring the distributed running of containers.\\nThe cluster\'s main components\\n1. The control plane\\n\\n- It is responsible for the management of the cluster.\\n\\n- It includes the `kube-apiserver`, `etcd`, the `controller-manager` and the `scheduler`.\\n\\n- It accepts the commands and distributes the load between the nodes.\\n\\n2. The worker nodes\\n\\n- They run the containers in the form of pods.\\n\\n- They include the `kubelet`, the `kube-proxy` and a container runtime (Docker, containerd, for example).\\n\\n- They communicate with the `control plane` in order to receive instructions.\\n\\nHow does a cluster work?\\n1. The developer creates a description of the desired state (\\"run 3 copies of the service\\", for example).\\n2. Kubernetes distributes the containers between the cluster\'s nodes.\\n3. The containers are run in pods, which ensure their work.\\n4. Kubernetes watches their state automatically and balances the load.\\nAn example:\\nThe creation of a simple Minikube cluster for local testing:\\n\\nThe output:\\n\\nNow we have one node working in the cluster.\\nA Kubernetes cluster unites several nodes for the management of containers. It makes applications fault-tolerant and flexible, distributing their running between the servers.","minikube start\\nkubectl get nodes\\n\\nNAME       STATUS   ROLES    AGE   VERSION\\nminikube   Ready    master   5m    v1.28.0"],[3030,"A node is a server in a Kubernetes cluster that runs containers. It can be physical or virtual. On every node the `kubelet` agent works, which manages the pods and interacts with the cluster\'s control plane. The nodes are united into a cluster for working together and for the balancing of the load.","In Kubernetes a node is the main computational element on which the containers are run. The nodes can be physical machines (bare metal) or virtual ones (in the AWS, GCP or Azure cloud, for example).\\nThe kinds of node:\\n1. A control plane node\\n\\n- It manages the cluster.\\n\\n- It includes the components: the `kube-apiserver`, the `controller-manager`, the `scheduler`.\\n\\n- It normally runs no containers, but distributes them over the worker nodes.\\n\\n2. A worker node\\n\\n- It runs the containers in the form of pods.\\n\\n- It includes:\\n\\n- the `kubelet` - the agent for managing the pods.\\n\\n- the `kube-proxy` - the routing of the traffic.\\n\\n- A container runtime (Docker, containerd, CRI-O).\\n\\nHow does a node work?\\n\\n- Kubernetes receives the command to deploy a pod.\\n\\n- The `scheduler` assigns the pod to one of the nodes.\\n\\n- The `kubelet` starts the containers inside the pod.\\n\\n- The `kube-proxy` configures the network for the interaction between the pods.\\n\\nAn example:\\nLet us look at the list of the nodes in the cluster:\\n\\nAn example of the output:\\n\\nThat means that there are two worker nodes in the cluster.\\nKubernetes\'s nodes are the servers on which the containerized applications work. The worker nodes run the pods, and the control plane node coordinates their work.","kubectl get nodes\\n\\nNAME         STATUS   ROLES    AGE   VERSION\\nworker-1     Ready    worker   2d    v1.28.0\\nworker-2     Ready    worker   2d    v1.28.0"],[3031,"A pod is the smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes, representing one or several containers working together. The containers inside a pod share the network and the file system, which lets them interact with one another. Kubernetes manages the pods, assigning them to nodes and ensuring their work.","In Kubernetes a pod is the main unit of running, which encapsulates one or several containers. If an application consists of several containers that have to work together (a web server and a proxy cache, for example), they can be united into one pod.\\nA pod\'s particulars:\\n\\n- A single network - all of the containers inside a pod use one IP address.\\n\\n- A shared disk - the containers can use the file system jointly.\\n\\n- Shared management - Kubernetes manages the pods rather than the individual containers.\\n\\nAn example of a pod\'s manifest:\\n\\nThat pod runs a container with `nginx`, which listens on port `80`.\\nConclusion:\\nA pod is the basic unit of deployment in Kubernetes, uniting containers into a logical group. Kubernetes manages the pods, ensuring their work and their interaction.","apiVersion: v1\\nkind: Pod\\nmetadata:\\n    name: my-pod\\nspec:\\n    containers:\\n    - name: app-container\\n      image: nginx\\n      ports:\\n      - containerPort: 80"],[3032,"The control plane is the set of components that manage a Kubernetes cluster. It includes the API Server, etcd, the Scheduler and the Controller Manager. These components are responsible for the handling of the requests, for the storage of the data about the cluster\'s state, for the distribution of the loads and for the control of the nodes\' work.","The control plane is Kubernetes\'s central part, managing the whole system. It accepts the commands, handles them and watches over their running.\\nThe main components:\\n\\n- The API Server - the point of entry into Kubernetes; it handles the commands (`kubectl`, the `API`).\\n\\n- etcd - the distributed storage of the cluster\'s configurations and states.\\n\\n- The Scheduler - it distributes the pods over the nodes, choosing the optimal place.\\n\\n- The Controller Manager - it manages the processes that maintain the system\'s state.\\n\\n- The Cloud Controller Manager - it interacts with the cloud providers (if the cluster is in a cloud).\\n\\nHow does that work?\\n\\n- The developer sends a command (`kubectl apply -f pod.yaml`).\\n\\n- The API Server accepts the request and writes it into `etcd`.\\n\\n- The Scheduler determines on which node the pod is to be run.\\n\\n- The kubelet (on the node) receives the command and starts the container.\\n\\nThe control plane is Kubernetes\'s \\"brain\\", which manages the cluster. It coordinates the nodes\' work, plans the deployment of the pods and watches the system\'s state.",null],[3033,"The API Server is Kubernetes\'s central component, which handles the users\' commands and interacts with the cluster\'s other components. It accepts the requests from `kubectl`, from the controllers and from the services, and then changes the cluster\'s state.","The Kubernetes API Server (`kube-apiserver`) is the main point of entry for managing the cluster.\\nThe main functions:\\n\\n- It handles the API requests (`kubectl`, `Helm`, the `Dashboard`).\\n\\n- It passes the commands to the other components (the `Scheduler`, the `Controller Manager`).\\n\\n- It holds the data in `etcd`.\\n\\nIt authorizes and authenticates the requests.\\nAn example of a request to the API Server:\\n\\nThe API Server receives that request, requests the data in `etcd` and returns the list of the pods.\\nThe API Server is Kubernetes\'s main interface of management, accepting the commands and distributing them between the system\'s components.","kubectl get pods"],[3034,"etcd is a distributed, reliable key-value storage in which Kubernetes holds all of the information about the cluster. It guarantees the consistency of the data and ensures fault tolerance.","`etcd` is a highly available storage, used by Kubernetes for holding the cluster\'s configuration.\\nThe main functions:\\n\\n- It holds the information about the pods, the nodes and the configurations.\\n\\n- It ensures the consistency of the data.\\n\\n- It supports fault tolerance (it is replicated between the nodes).\\n\\nAn example:\\nLet us look at `etcd`\'s content:\\n\\nWhen a node fails, Kubernetes restores its state on the basis of `etcd`\'s data.\\n`etcd` is Kubernetes\'s storage of data, which guarantees the preservation and the consistency of the information about the cluster.","kubectl get etcd"],[3035,"CI/CD (Continuous Integration / Continuous Delivery) is a development methodology that automates the process of testing and deploying code. Developers regularly bring changes into a shared repository, where the code is checked and deployed automatically. That helps release new features and fixes faster with minimal risks.","CI/CD is an approach to software development that includes two key processes:\\n\\n- Continuous Integration (CI) — the automatic combining of every developer\'s code in a shared repository with a check that it works.\\n\\n- Continuous Delivery (CD) — the automatic preparation of the code for deployment to production without the need for manual intervention.\\n\\nAn example of a CI/CD process:\\n\\n- A developer makes changes in the code and pushes them to the repository.\\n\\n- Automated tests check the code for errors.\\n\\n- If the tests pass successfully, the code is automatically built and deployed to a test server.\\n\\n- When Continuous Deployment is used, the code is also rolled out to production automatically.\\n\\nCI/CD makes the development process simpler, reduces the number of errors and speeds up the release of new versions.",null],[3036,"CI/CD speeds up the development process through the automation of testing and deployment. That reduces the likelihood of errors, increases the stability of the system and lets you deliver new features to users faster.","The main advantages of CI/CD:\\n\\n- Fast software delivery — the automatic deployment of code reduces the time between writing a feature and its availability to users.\\n\\n- A reduction in the number of errors — automated testing lets you find bugs earlier, while still at the development stage.\\n\\n- Stability and reliability — the code passes through stages of checking before it reaches production.\\n\\n- Better collaboration — developers can work in parallel, integrating code regularly without conflicts.\\n\\n- The minimisation of manual labour — automation reduces the need for manual tests and deployment.\\n\\nUsing CI/CD lets you speed up and simplify development while ensuring higher-quality code.",null],[3037,"Continuous Integration (CI) is the process in which developers add code to a shared repository often. Every commit is tested automatically, so that errors are revealed at once and eliminated.","Continuous Integration includes:\\n\\n- Bringing in changes regularly — developers push code to the repository several times a day.\\n\\n- Automatic testing — the CI system runs the tests after every commit.\\n\\n- Automatic building — if the tests succeed, the code is built for further use.\\n\\nAn example of a CI process:\\n1. A developer commits code to a Git repository.\\n2. The CI system (Jenkins, GitHub Actions or GitLab CI, for example) runs the tests.\\n3. If the tests pass, the code is built and becomes available for a subsequent deployment.",null],[3038,"Continuous Delivery (CD) is the process in which the code, after a successful check, is automatically prepared for release to production. It stays in a stable state and can be deployed at any moment.","Continuous Delivery includes:\\n\\n- Automatic deployment to test environments — after CI the code is handed over to a test server.\\n\\n- Constant readiness to deploy — the code can be deployed to production at any moment without additional corrections.\\n\\n- The minimisation of the human factor — the deployment process becomes predictable and reliable.\\n\\nAn example of a CD process:\\n\\n- The code passes through CI and testing.\\n\\n- If all of the tests succeed, it is automatically deployed to staging (pre-production).\\n\\n- Developers and testers check the new release.\\n\\n- Where necessary, the team manually confirms the deployment to production.\\n\\nContinuous Delivery reduces the risks during deployment by ensuring constant readiness for release.",null],[3039,"Continuous Integration (CI) checks and tests the code before it is combined with the main branch. Continuous Delivery (CD) goes further and automatically prepares the code for deployment to production.","The main differences:\\nContinuous Integration (CI):\\n\\n- Developers commit code to the repository often.\\n\\n- Automatic testing is started after every change.\\n\\n- Reduces the likelihood of errors thanks to the early checking of the code.\\n\\nContinuous Delivery (CD):\\n\\n- The code is automatically built and deployed to a test server.\\n\\n- Prepares the code for a fast deployment to production.\\n\\n- Developers can deploy the software at any time without additional changes.\\n\\nAn example of CI/CD at work:\\n\\n- A developer makes a commit on GitHub.\\n\\n- The CI system runs the tests and checks the code.\\n\\n- If the tests pass, the CD system deploys the code to staging.\\n\\n- After the final testing the code is moved to production, either manually or automatically.\\n\\nCI guarantees that the code always works correctly, while CD ensures that it is ready to deploy.",null],[3040,"A build pipeline is a set of automated steps the code passes through before deployment. It includes building, testing and preparation for deployment, guaranteeing that only working code reaches production","A build pipeline helps automate the process of turning source code into a product ready for deployment. It usually consists of the following stages:\\n\\n- Build — the compilation of the source code into an executable file.\\n\\n- Testing — automatic checks for errors and regressions.\\n\\n- Code Analysis — a check of the style and the quality of the code (with SonarQube, for example).\\n\\n- Packaging — the preparation of the artefacts for deployment.\\n\\n- Staging — deployment to a test environment.\\n\\nA build pipeline automates the process of preparing the code, reducing the likelihood of errors and speeding up development.",null],[3041,"A deployment pipeline is the process of deploying code to production automatically. It includes checking, testing and deployment to the live environment, minimising the risks.","The main stages of a deployment pipeline:\\n1. The preparation of the code — building and testing in the build pipeline.\\n2. Placement in a test environment — a check before the production deployment.\\n3. Additional testing — load testing, integration tests.\\n4. Deployment to production — the automatic or manual process of moving the code onto the live server.\\n5. Monitoring and rollback — a check that it works after the deployment, with the ability to roll the changes back quickly.\\nA deployment pipeline lets you release new versions of software safely and quickly, minimising errors.",null],[3042,"Version control is a system that lets you track changes in code, manage different versions of files and have several developers work on a project at the same time.","Version control helps developers:\\n\\n- Track changes — see who changed the code and when.\\n\\n- Work in teams — several developers can edit the code in parallel without conflicts.\\n\\n- Roll back to previous versions — if an error has appeared in the code, you can return the working version.\\n\\n- Use branching — create separate versions of the code for work on new features.\\n\\nExamples of version control systems:\\n\\n- Git (used with GitHub, GitLab, Bitbucket)\\n\\n- Subversion (SVN)\\n\\n- Mercurial\\n\\nVersion control makes joint development simpler, reduces the likelihood of losing code and makes managing projects easier.",null],[3043,"The main components of CI/CD:\\n\\n- Source Control — the management of the source code (Git, SVN).\\n\\n- Build Automation — the automatic building of the code.\\n\\n- Testing — running automated tests.\\n\\n- Deployment Automation — deploying the code to test and production environments.","1. Source Control (version control)\\n\\n- The use of Git or other code management systems.\\n\\n- Lets you track changes and prevent conflicts.\\n\\n2. Build Automation\\n\\n- The generation of executable files or containers.\\n\\n- Tools: Maven, Gradle, Docker.\\n\\n3. Testing (automated testing)\\n\\n- Unit tests, integration tests, load tests.\\n\\n- Tools: JUnit, Selenium.\\n\\n4. Deployment Automation\\n\\n- The delivery of the code to staging and to production.\\n\\n- Tools: Kubernetes, Helm, Terraform.\\n\\nA CI/CD pipeline automates the whole development cycle from writing the code to deploying it, speeding up the release of new versions.",null],[3044,"A build is the process of transforming source code into an executable file.\\nA release is the deployment of the built code to production.","The main differences:\\nBuild:\\n\\n- The compilation of the source code into an executable file or a container.\\n\\n- The process is automated (with Jenkins, for example).\\n\\n- Started on every change of the code.\\n\\nRelease:\\n\\n- The delivery of the built code to a server or a cloud platform.\\n\\n- Can be a manual or an automatic process.\\n\\n- Happens less often than a build, since it requires additional testing.\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nA build is the preparation of the executable file, while a release is its deployment to production.","# Build the project\\nmvn package  \\n\\n# Deploy to production\\nkubectl apply -f deployment.yaml"],[3045,"A Jenkinsfile is a text file in which the stages of a CI/CD pipeline are described: building, testing, deployment. It lets you manage Jenkins processes with code, ensuring transparency and repeatability.","A Jenkinsfile is a configuration file written in Groovy, which is used to describe pipelines in Jenkins. It lets you automate processes such as:\\n\\n- Build — the compilation of the code and the generation of the artefacts.\\n\\n- Test — running automatic tests.\\n\\n- Deploy — sending the code to a test or production environment.\\n\\nAn example of a Jenkinsfile:\\n\\nA Jenkinsfile makes managing CI/CD in Jenkins simpler by letting you keep the configuration in the code repository.","pipeline {\\n    agent any\\n    stages {\\n        stage(\'Build\') {\\n            steps {\\n                sh \'mvn package\'\\n            }\\n        }\\n        stage(\'Test\') {\\n            steps {\\n                sh \'mvn test\'\\n            }\\n        }\\n        stage(\'Deploy\') {\\n            steps {\\n                sh \'kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml\'\\n            }\\n        }\\n    }\\n}"],[3046,"A container is a lightweight, isolated package containing an application\'s code, its dependencies and its settings. It lets you run applications independently of the environment, making their deployment and scaling simpler.","A container is an isolated environment in which an application runs independently of the operating system and of other applications. The main characteristics of containers:\\n\\n- Isolation — every application runs in its own surroundings without conflicting with the others.\\n\\n- Lightness — containers take up fewer resources than virtual machines.\\n\\n- Portability — they work identically in any environment (locally, in the cloud, on a server).\\n\\nAn example of running a container with Nginx:\\n\\nContainers make deploying applications simpler, more convenient and more reliable.","docker run -d -p 8080:80 nginx"],[3047,"Blue-green deployment is a deployment strategy in which two environments (blue and green) run in parallel. The new version of the application is deployed into the green environment, tested, and then all of the traffic is switched over to it, turning it into the new production environment.","Blue-green deployment helps minimise downtime and the risks during deployment:\\n\\n- Blue (the blue environment) — the current production version of the application.\\n\\n- Green (the green environment) — the new version, deployed in parallel.\\n\\n- Switching the traffic — after testing, the traffic is directed to the green environment.\\n\\n- Rollback — if errors are discovered in the new version, you can return to the blue environment quickly.\\n\\nAn example of switching the traffic in Kubernetes:\\n\\nBlue-green deployment lets you deploy new versions safely without downtime for the users.","apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1\\nkind: Ingress\\nmetadata:\\n  name: my-app\\nspec:\\n  rules:\\n    - host: myapp.com\\n      http:\\n        paths:\\n          - path: /\\n            backend:\\n              service:\\n                name: my-app-green\\n                port:\\n                  number: 80"],[3048,"Canary deployment is a deployment strategy in which the new version of the application is first started for a small part of the users. If the testing passes successfully, it gradually replaces the old version.","Canary deployment reduces the risks of deployment thanks to a gradual update:\\n\\n- A small percentage of the users — the new version first receives 5-10% of the traffic.\\n\\n- Monitoring and testing — errors and performance metrics are tracked.\\n\\n- Gradual deployment — if everything works well, the new version receives more traffic.\\n\\n- Rollback — if problems are discovered, the traffic is redirected back to the old version.\\n\\nAn example in Kubernetes with Istio:\\n\\nCanary deployment lets you test updates without the risk of mass failures.","apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1alpha3\\nkind: VirtualService\\nmetadata:\\n  name: canary-deployment\\nspec:\\n  hosts:\\n    - myapp.com\\n  http:\\n    - route:\\n        - destination:\\n            host: my-app-v1\\n          weight: 80\\n        - destination:\\n            host: my-app-v2\\n          weight: 20"],[3049,"Code coverage is a measure showing which part of the code is tested by automatic tests. It is expressed as a percentage: the higher the measure, the lower the risk of bugs appearing.","Code coverage measures how fully the code is covered by tests. The main types of coverage:\\n\\n- Statement Coverage — how many lines of code were executed during the tests.\\n\\n- Branch Coverage — checks whether every branch of the conditions (`if-else`) is tested.\\n\\n- Function Coverage — measures whether every function in the code is tested.\\n\\nAn example of checking test coverage in Java with JaCoCo:\\n\\nHigh code coverage reduces the risk of errors, but 100% coverage does not guarantee the absence of bugs — what matters is testing the critical paths of the code.","mvn clean test jacoco:report"],[3050,"Infrastructure as Code (IaC) is a method of managing and deploying IT infrastructure by means of code rather than the manual configuration of servers. That lets you automate and version the environment and keep it uniform.","IaC replaces the manual configuration of servers, networks and other components with program code. The main advantages:\\n\\n- Automation — the infrastructure is deployed quickly and without errors.\\n\\n- Consistency — the code guarantees an identical configuration in every environment.\\n\\n- Versioning — changes in the infrastructure can be tracked through Git.\\n\\nAn example of the configuration of a virtual machine in Terraform:\\n\\nIaC makes the infrastructure repeatable, manageable and easy to change through code.","resource \\"aws_instance\\" \\"web\\" {\\n    ami           = \\"ami-123456\\"\\n    instance_type = \\"t2.micro\\"\\n}"],[3051,"GitOps is a method of managing infrastructure and applications through Git repositories. Changes in the infrastructure code are applied automatically through CI/CD processes.","GitOps uses Git as the single source of truth for deploying infrastructure and applications. The main principles:\\n\\n- Declarativeness — the infrastructure is described in code (in Kubernetes YAML files, for example).\\n\\n- Automatic application — changes in Git are applied in the environment automatically.\\n\\n- Version control — every change is tracked and easily rolled back.\\n\\nAn example of GitOps in Kubernetes with ArgoCD:\\n\\nGitOps makes managing infrastructure simpler by making it more predictable and safer.","apiVersion: argoproj.io/v1alpha1\\nkind: Application\\nmetadata:\\n    name: my-app\\nspec:\\n    source:\\n        repoURL: \'https://github.com/example/repo.git\'\\n        path: \'deploy\'\\n        targetRevision: HEAD"],[3052,"A microservice architecture is a way of developing software in which the application is broken up into independent services that interact through an API. That improves scalability and the flexibility of development.","Microservices are small independent services that carry out specific tasks. The main principles:\\n\\n- The separation of the logic — every microservice is responsible for one function.\\n\\n- Independent deployment — services can be updated without stopping the whole application.\\n\\n- Flexibility of technologies — different microservices can use different programming languages.\\n\\nAn example of the interaction of microservices through a REST API:\\n\\nA microservice architecture lets you build flexible, scalable applications, but it calls for well-thought-out management of the services.","GET /orders/123\\n{\\n  \\"orderId\\": 123,\\n  \\"status\\": \\"shipped\\"\\n}"],[3053,"Chaos Engineering is the practice of introducing artificial failures into a system in order to check how resilient it is. That lets you reveal weak spots in advance and prevent major outages.","Chaos Engineering helps prepare a system for real failures. The main steps:\\n1. Determining the system\'s normal state — which metrics are considered stable?\\n2. Introducing failures — switching off a server or slowing the network down, for example.\\n3. Monitoring the reaction — how does the system behave during a failure?\\n4. Conclusions and corrections — reworking the system in order to increase its resilience.\\nAn example of the use of Chaos Monkey (Netflix):\\n\\nChaos Engineering helps companies build fault-tolerant systems that are ready for unexpected failures.","chaosmonkey attack --target=service --region=us-east-1"],[3054,"Observability is a system\'s ability to provide metrics, logs and traces, so that errors can be found and corrected quickly.","Observability includes three key components:\\n\\n- Metrics — numeric measures of the system\'s operation (CPU load or request latency, for example).\\n\\n- Logs — textual records about the system\'s operation (errors, requests, events).\\n\\n- Tracing — the tracking of requests inside a distributed system.\\n\\nAn example of a metrics query in Prometheus:\\n\\nObservability helps you find and eliminate problems in complex systems quickly.","curl http://localhost:9090/api/v1/query?query=up"],[3055,"Building and deployment are organised through CI (GitHub Actions, GitLab CI, Bitrise, Jenkins), where the steps include: cloning the repository, installing the dependencies (`CocoaPods`, SPM), building through `xcodebuild`, running the tests, packaging the `.ipa` and publishing to TestFlight / App Store Connect by means of `fastlane`.","The CI tool:\\n- The choice of platform (CircleCI, Bitrise, GitHub Actions).\\n- Setting up the workflow or the pipeline.\\n\\nThe build steps:\\n- `pod install` / `swift package resolve`.\\n- `xcodebuild clean build -workspace … -scheme … -sdk iphoneos -configuration Release`.\\n- `xcodebuild test …` to run `XCTest`.\\n\\nThe artefact:\\n- Creating the `xcarchive` and exporting the `.ipa`:\\n\\nDeployment:\\n- `fastlane` lanes:\\n\\n- Publication to App Store Connect, screenshots, metadata.\\n\\nAutomation and notifications:\\n- On a successful build, notifications are sent to Slack or by email.\\n- On a failure — a report to the issue tracker.\\n\\nConclusion:\\nA well-configured CI/CD flow saves time, guarantees the quality of the code and makes delivering new versions of the application simpler.","xcodebuild -exportArchive -archivePath MyApp.xcarchive -exportOptionsPlist ExportOptions.plist\\n\\nlane :beta do\\n  build_app(scheme: \\"MyApp\\")\\n  upload_to_testflight\\nend"],[3056,"Front-end developers use Docker for:\\n\\n- Running a single development environment (Node.js and the dependencies, for example).\\n\\n- Testing the application in isolated surroundings.\\n\\n- Making deployment together with the back end simpler.","Docker lets you package an application and all of its dependencies into a container that works identically on any system.\\nAn example of its use:\\n\\n- Development:\\n\\n- A `Dockerfile` is created to install Node.js and the dependencies:\\n\\n- Running it:\\n\\n- Testing:\\nTests can be run in the container, so as to avoid problems with different versions of Node.js.\\n\\n- Deployment:\\nThe front end is built into static files (through `npm run build`, for example), which are then placed in an Nginx container:\\n\\nDocker Compose is also used to run the front end and the back end together:\\n\\nThat makes teamwork and deployment simpler.","FROM node:18\\nWORKDIR /app\\nCOPY package.json .\\nRUN npm install\\nCOPY . .\\nCMD [\\"npm\\", \\"run\\", \\"dev\\"]\\n\\ndocker build -t frontend-app .\\ndocker run -p 3000:3000 frontend-app\\n\\nFROM nginx:alpine\\nCOPY dist /usr/share/nginx/html\\n\\nservices:\\n  frontend:\\n    build: ./frontend\\n    ports: [\\"3000:3000\\"]\\n  backend:\\n    build: ./backend\\n    ports: [\\"5000:5000\\"]"],[3057,"CI/CD can be set up by means of systems of the GitHub Actions, GitLab CI or Jenkins kind, where the build, test and deployment steps are defined.","CI (Continuous Integration):\\n\\n- Checking the code (lint, tests, build)\\n\\n- Configured through a `.yaml` file (`.github/workflows/...`, for example)\\n\\n- Started automatically on a push or a PR\\n\\nCD (Continuous Delivery/Deployment):\\n\\n- The delivery of the artefacts (a Docker image, a binary)\\n\\n- Automatic deployment to staging/production\\n\\nAn example of the CI/CD steps:\\n\\n- Checkout of the code\\n\\n- Installation of the dependencies\\n\\n- Building\\n\\n- Running the tests\\n\\n- Building and pushing the Docker image\\n\\n- Deployment to the server / Kubernetes",null],[3058,"Every microservice should have its own `Dockerfile`, minimal dependencies and an independent image. Version tags are used, along with the configuration of environment variables and CI/CD for automatic building and delivery.","1. The separation of the services:\\n\\n- Every microservice is a separate repository or folder.\\n\\n- Its own `Dockerfile` with a minimal environment (`python:slim`, `alpine`).\\n\\n2. The use of versioning:\\n\\n- The image tags should include version numbers (`v1.0.0`, `latest`).\\n\\n3. The management of the dependencies:\\n\\n- Use virtual environments or a `requirements.txt`.\\n\\n- The packages should be \\"frozen\\" (through `pip freeze`).\\n\\n4. Secrets and configurations:\\n\\n- Do not keep them in the image; use environment variables and a Kubernetes ConfigMap/Secret.\\n\\n5. Integration with CI/CD:\\n\\n- The automatic building of the images after a commit.\\n\\n- Delivery to a Docker Registry and deployment to the cluster\\n\\nWhen to use it:\\nContainerisation is necessary for scalable, independent microservices, especially in Kubernetes and cloud infrastructure.",null],[3059,"CI/CD for a backend application is set up by means of tools of the GitHub Actions, Jenkins or GitLab CI kind. The process includes building, testing and deploying the application, often using containers and orchestration (Kubernetes, for example).","CI/CD (Continuous Integration/Continuous Deployment) automates the building, testing and deployment of applications, shortening the time it takes to deliver changes.\\n\\n- The stages of CI/CD:\\n\\n- Building: The compilation of the code and the creation of the artefacts (Docker images, for example).\\n\\n- Testing: Automatic tests (unit, integration).\\n\\n- Deployment: The publication of the application to a server or to the cloud.\\n\\n- The tools:\\n\\n- GitHub Actions: Pipelines configured through YAML.\\n\\n- Jenkins: A flexible tool for complex pipelines.\\n\\n- GitLab CI: Integrated with GitLab, supports containers.\\n\\n- CircleCI: Convenient for cloud projects.\\n\\n- An example:\\nCI/CD for a Go application:\\n\\n- The code is pushed to the repository.\\n\\n- GitHub Actions builds the Docker image, runs the tests and deploys to Kubernetes.\\n\\nWhen to use it:\\nCI/CD is necessary for the automation of development, especially in teams where releases are frequent.\\nConclusion:\\nCI/CD speeds up development and raises the quality of the code. GitHub Actions and Jenkins are popular tools for automating backend applications.","name: CI/CD\\non: [push]\\njobs:\\n  build:\\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\\n    steps:\\n    - uses: actions/checkout@v3\\n    - name: Build Docker image\\n      run: docker build -t app:latest .\\n    - name: Run tests\\n      run: go test ./..."],[3060,"For CI/CD, tools of the Jenkins, GitLab CI, GitHub Actions, CircleCI and TeamCity kind are used most often. They help automate the process of building, testing and delivering an application, reducing the number of errors and speeding up the release. These systems integrate with code repositories and let you construct pipelines.","CI/CD (Continuous Integration / Continuous Delivery) is the practice in which:\\n\\n- CI (continuous integration) — automatically builds and tests the code on every commit.\\n\\n- CD (continuous delivery/deployment) — automatically delivers the code to test and production environments.\\n\\nThe most widespread tools:\\n\\n- Jenkins\\n\\n- An open system with a multitude of plugins.\\n\\n- Flexible, but calls for manual configuration.\\n\\n- The scripts are written in Groovy (Pipeline DSL).\\n\\n- GitLab CI/CD\\n\\n- Built into GitLab.\\n\\n- Convenient for teams already working in GitLab.\\n\\n- The configuration is described in a `.gitlab-ci.yml`.\\n\\n- GitHub Actions\\n\\n- Suits projects on GitHub.\\n\\n- Simple to set up, with many ready-made actions.\\n\\n- The configuration is in YAML files.\\n\\n- CircleCI\\n\\n- A cloud CI/CD with simple configuration.\\n\\n- Works well with Docker and Kubernetes.\\n\\n- TeamCity\\n\\n- A commercial product from JetBrains.\\n\\n- A convenient UI, integration with the IDE.\\n\\nAn example of a pipeline (GitHub Actions):\\n\\nWhen to use which:\\n\\n- Jenkins — for flexible custom solutions.\\n\\n- GitHub/GitLab — if you use the corresponding hosting.\\n\\n- CircleCI/TeamCity — for integration with Docker and complex scenarios.","name: Java CI\\non:\\n  push:\\n    branches: [main]\\njobs:\\n  build:\\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\\n    steps:\\n    - uses: actions/checkout@v2\\n    - name: Setup JDK\\n      uses: actions/setup-java@v3\\n      with:\\n        java-version: \'17\'\\n    - name: Build with Maven\\n      run: mvn clean install"],[3061,"The typical checks:\\n\\n- Static analysis: `flake8` (code style), `mypy` (types).\\n\\n- Tests: `pytest` (unit tests), `coverage` (coverage).\\n\\n- Security: `bandit` (Python vulnerabilities).\\nThese checks are run automatically on every commit.","A CI/CD pipeline for a Python project includes the stages:\\n\\n- The static analysis of the code:\\n\\n- `flake8`: Checks the style (PEP8) and simple errors.\\n\\n- `mypy`: Type checking.\\n\\n- Running the tests:\\n\\n- `pytest`: Unit tests + integration ones.\\n\\n- `coverage`: A coverage report. Blocks the deployment if it is < 80%.\\n\\n- The security check:\\n\\n- `bandit`: Scans the code for vulnerabilities.\\n\\nAn example of a `.gitlab-ci.yml`:\\n\\nConclusion:\\nAutomatic checks save time on review and prevent bugs.","flake8 . --max-line-length=120\\n\\nmypy src/\\n\\npytest tests/ --cov=src\\n\\nbandit -r src/\\n\\nstages:\\n  - test\\n\\nflake8:\\n  stage: test\\n  script:\\n    - flake8 . --max-line-length=120\\n\\nmypy:\\n  script:\\n    - mypy src/\\n\\npytest:\\n  script:\\n    - pytest tests/ --cov=src --cov-fail-under=80"],[3062,"The main tools:\\n\\n- Jenkins - the orchestration of pipelines\\n\\n- GitLab CI - built-in pipelines\\n\\n- GitHub Actions - cloud workflows","The process of running automated tests:\\n\\n- Initiation:\\n\\n- A push to the repository\\n\\n- On a schedule (nightly tests)\\n\\n- Manually through the UI\\n\\n- The stages of the pipeline:\\n\\n- The artefacts:\\n\\n- Allure/HTML reports\\n\\n- Execution logs\\n\\n- Screenshots for UI tests","pipeline {\\n    stages {\\n        stage(\'Test\') {\\n            steps {\\n                sh \'pytest tests/\'\\n            }\\n        }\\n    }\\n}"],[3063,"- The stages in .gitlab-ci.yml:\\n\\n- Running the unit tests on every commit.\\n\\n- E2E tests before the deployment.\\n\\n- Artefacts: Allure reports.","An example of a GitLab CI configuration\\n\\nConclusion:\\nIntegrating tests into CI/CD speeds up the feedback and raises reliability.","stages:\\n  - test\\n  - deploy\\n\\nunit_tests:\\n  stage: test\\n  script:\\n    - pytest tests/unit/\\n\\ne2e_tests:\\n  stage: test\\n  only:\\n    - master\\n  script:\\n    - pytest tests/e2e/\\n  artifacts:\\n    paths:\\n      - allure-results/"],[3064,"- Unit tests – a fast check of individual modules.\\n\\n- Integration tests – a check of the interaction of the components.\\n\\n- E2E tests – complete user scenarios.\\n\\n- Security tests – a check for vulnerabilities.","1. Unit tests\\n\\n- When they are run: On every commit.\\n\\n- Tools: pytest (Python), JUnit (Java).\\n\\n- An example for GitLab CI:\\n\\n2. Integration tests\\n\\n- When they are run: After successful unit tests.\\n\\n- What they check: Work with the database, the API, external services.\\n\\n- Tools: Testcontainers, Postman.\\n\\n3. E2E tests\\n\\n- When they are run: Before the deployment to staging.\\n\\n- Tools: Selenium, Cypress.\\n\\n- An example:\\n\\n4. Security tests\\n\\n- When they are run: Nightly runs or before a release.\\n\\n- Tools: OWASP ZAP, SonarQube.\\n\\nConclusion:\\nThe stages are arranged on the principle of the testing pyramid – from fast unit tests to comprehensive E2E checks.","unit_test:\\n  stage: test\\n  script:\\n    - pytest tests/unit/\\n\\ne2e_test:\\n  stage: test\\n  script:\\n    - pytest tests/e2e/\\n  only:\\n    - master"],[3065,"Integration tests are often excluded from CI/CD because of:\\n\\n- Their long execution (they slow the pipeline down).\\n\\n- Their dependence on external services (instability, mocks).\\n\\n- The high cost of maintaining them (they require complex environments).","1. Alternative approaches\\n\\n- Running them on a schedule: Nightly runs, for example.\\n\\n- Selective running: Only for critical changes (a manual trigger).\\n\\n- A pre-production environment: Testing before a release.\\n\\n2. Compensating measures\\n\\n- Strengthened unit tests with mocks.\\n\\n- Contract testing (Pact).\\n\\nConclusion:\\nIntegration tests matter, but their place is closer to the production environment rather than in a fast CI.",null],[3066,"Profiling is the process of measuring the performance of code. In Go it is done through net/http/pprof, which lets you collect metrics for the CPU, memory, locks and so on.","What can be profiled:\\n\\n- The CPU\\n\\n- Memory allocation (heap)\\n\\n- Locks (blocking)\\n\\n- The use of goroutines\\n\\nHow to use it:\\nAdd pprof to your application:\\n\\nThe available profiles:\\n\\n- /debug/pprof/heap\\n\\n- /debug/pprof/profile\\n\\n- /debug/pprof/goroutine\\n\\nThey can be analysed through go tool pprof:\\n\\nConclusion:\\nProfiling is the key to optimisation. Use it when performance falls, when memory leaks and when resource consumption is high.","import _ \\"net/http/pprof\\"\\nimport \\"net/http\\"\\n\\nfunc main() {\\n    go http.ListenAndServe(\\":6060\\", nil)\\n}\\n\\ngo tool pprof <http://localhost:6060/debug/pprof/profile>"],[3067,"Test environments ensure the isolation of the development stages. Dev is for active development, QA for testing, Prod is the live one. Every environment has its own configuration, databases and external services. Deployment happens sequentially through the pipeline. The key principles: identical configurations, versioning, and minimal differences between the environments.","The organisation of test environments is critically important for the stability of the development process.\\nThe typical environments and their purpose:\\n\\n- Development (Dev):\\n\\n- For the developers\' everyday work\\n\\n- Fast deployment, unstable versions are possible\\n\\n- Local databases, mock services\\n\\n- Quality Assurance (QA/Test):\\n\\n- For manual and automatic testing\\n\\n- Stable versions, close to production\\n\\n- Isolated test data\\n\\n- Staging/Pre-production:\\n\\n- As close to production as possible\\n\\n- For the final testing and for demonstrations\\n\\n- The same configurations and infrastructure as in prod\\n\\n- Production (Prod):\\n\\n- The live environment for real users\\n\\n- Maximum stability and monitoring\\n\\n- A strict deployment process\\n\\nThe principles of organisation:\\nThe identity of the environments:\\n\\n- Identical versions of the software and the dependencies\\n\\n- Similar hardware characteristics\\n\\n- The minimisation of differences in the configuration\\n\\nConfiguration management:\\n\\nThe deployment process:\\n\\n- Development in feature branches\\n\\n- A merge into develop → automatic deployment to Dev\\n\\n- The creation of a release → deployment to QA\\n\\n- After the testing → deployment to Staging → Production\\n\\nMonitoring and logging:\\n\\n- Centralised logging for every environment\\n\\n- Metrics and alerting\\n\\n- Tools for diagnosing problems\\n\\nConclusion: The correct organisation of the environments reduces the risks and speeds up the delivery of new functionality.","# application-dev.yml\\ndatabase:\\n  url: jdbc:postgresql://localhost:5432/dev\\n\\n# application-qa.yml  \\ndatabase:\\n  url: jdbc:postgresql://qa-db:5432/qa\\n\\n# application-prod.yml\\ndatabase:\\n  url: jdbc:postgresql://prod-db:5432/prod"],[3068,"The delivery of code to a test environment is an automated or manual process that usually includes building the application from the repository, creating an artefact (a Docker image or a JAR file, for example), deploying it to the test server and applying the database migrations. Most often this process is orchestrated by a CI/CD pipeline","The delivery (deployment) process can vary depending on the project, but it usually follows a common scheme.\\n1. The preparation of the artefact:\\n\\n- Building: The CI server (Jenkins, for example) takes the code from a particular branch of the repository (`develop`, for example), starts the build and runs the unit tests.\\n\\n- The creation of the artefact: The result of the build is an artefact — the application\'s executable file. In modern approaches this is often a Docker image, which is placed in a registry (Docker Hub, for example).\\n\\n2. Deployment:\\n\\n- Updating the environment: On the test environment a script or a command is started (often from the CD part of the pipeline) that stops the old version of the application and starts the new one, using the fresh artefact.\\n\\n- Database migrations: If the structure of the database has changed in the new version, migration scripts are applied (by means of Flyway or Liquibase, for example).\\n\\n3. The validation of the delivery:\\n\\n- Health Checks: After the deployment the system can automatically check that the application has started correctly by sending a request to a health-check endpoint.\\n\\n- Running smoke tests: A fast set of critical tests is executed, in order to make sure that the main functionality works.\\n\\nWhen to use it:\\nThis process is repeated on every change of the code, so that the testers always have access to the current version of the application for checking.",null],[3069,"Development is the developers\' working environment, Staging is an almost complete copy of production for testing, and Production is the environment where real users work. The separation helps deploy changes safely.","Development environments: why Development, Staging and Production are needed\\nThe separation of the environments ensures quality control and the safety of introducing changes.\\n1. Development (DEV)\\n\\n- Used by developers to write and test code locally.\\n\\n- The data is fake, the configurations simplified.\\n\\n- Security restrictions are often switched off.\\n\\nAn example of DEV settings:\\n\\n2. Staging (STG)\\nStaging is a replica of Production, but without real users.\\n\\n- Used by QA, testers and analysts.\\n\\n- Migrations, integrations and performance are tested.\\n\\n- Ideally suited to checking releases.\\n\\n3. Production (PROD)\\nProduction is the live environment where the product runs.\\n\\n- Real data.\\n\\n- High requirements for stability and security.\\n\\n- Changes are deployed only after passing STG.\\n\\nAn example of PROD settings:\\n\\nWhy separate them?\\n\\n- A reduction in the risks.\\n\\n- Predictable releases.\\n\\n- The ability to test without affecting the users.\\n\\nSummary\\nDEV is for development, STG for checking, PROD for real work.","DEBUG = True\\nDATABASE_URL = \\"sqlite:///local.db\\"\\n\\nDEBUG = False\\nDATABASE_URL = \\"postgresql://...\\""],[3070,"Counter-type metrics in Prometheus always grow (or are zeroed on a restart), so in Grafana the graphs are usually built not from the “raw” values but from their derivatives — by means of the `rate()` or `increase()` functions. That lets you obtain, for example, the number of requests per second or the number of errors over an interval. In Grafana you set up queries against Prometheus (through PromQL), choose the time interval you need and build the graphs/panels. The counters are thus turned into clear measures of load and errors.","A `counter`-type metric is a basic type in Prometheus.\\nIt increases monotonically (it only grows) and can be reset when the process restarts. That suits counting events perfectly: requests, errors, tasks.\\nAn example of a counter in an application\\n\\nIn the metric we will receive lines of the form:\\n\\nWhy you cannot simply draw the “raw” values\\nIf you simply build a graph of `http_requests_total`, it will grow monotonically — which is not very useful.\\nWhat usually interests us is the rate of the events: requests per second, errors per minute and so on.\\nThe use of `rate()` and `increase()`\\nPromQL has two key functions:\\n\\n- `rate(counter[5m])` — the average rate of growth of the metric over the last 5 minutes;\\n\\n- `increase(counter[5m])` — the total increment over the interval.\\n\\nExamples of queries for Grafana:\\n\\n- Requests per second:\\n\\n- The number of 500 responses per minute:\\n\\nThe use in Grafana\\nIn Grafana, when creating a panel:\\n\\n- We choose the data source: Prometheus.\\n\\n- We enter the PromQL query, for example:\\n\\n- We set up the visualisation (graph, bars, single stat).\\n\\n- Optionally we add thresholds and alerts.\\n\\nA practical scenario\\n\\n- `http_requests_total` — the overall counter of requests.\\n\\n- The graphs in Grafana:\\n\\n- “Requests per second” → `sum(rate(http_requests_total[1m]))`\\n\\n- “5xx per minute” → `sum(increase(http_requests_total{status=~\\"5..\\"}[1m]))`\\n\\n- “Error rate %” → the ratio of the errors to the overall number of requests.\\n\\nConclusion\\nA counter on its own is just a number. The meaning appears when you use the `rate()` and `increase()` functions and build panels in Grafana on their basis. That is the standard way of obtaining understandable metrics of the “requests per second” and “errors per minute” kind.","from prometheus_client import Counter\\n\\nREQUESTS_TOTAL = Counter(\\n    \\"http_requests_total\\",\\n    \\"Total HTTP requests\\",\\n    [\\"method\\", \\"status\\"]\\n)\\n\\ndef handle_request(method, status):\\n    REQUESTS_TOTAL.labels(method=method, status=status).inc()\\n\\nhttp_requests_total{method=\\"GET\\",status=\\"200\\"}  1243\\nhttp_requests_total{method=\\"GET\\",status=\\"500\\"}  12\\n\\nrate(http_requests_total[5m])\\n\\nincrease(http_requests_total{status=\\"500\\"}[1m])\\n\\nsum(rate(http_requests_total[5m])) by (status)"],[3071,"In teamwork, Agile approaches are used, including processes such as Scrum, Kanban, XP and Lean. Agile helps teams work iteratively, adapt to changes and deliver value regularly. The main Agile patterns are the daily stand-up, sprint planning, the retrospective and backlog grooming. Code review, Git-flow, CI/CD and the practice of small incremental tasks are also actively applied. The Agile philosophy is aimed at flexibility, transparency and fast feedback.","Team software development is impossible without processes that ensure consistency, predictability and speed. Agile methodologies are a set of principles and practices that help teams interact effectively and adapt to changes.\\nThe main approaches to teamwork\\n1. Iterative development\\nThe project is broken up into small increments, which are delivered regularly.\\n2. Joint ownership of the code\\nEvery developer can work on any part of the project.\\nFor that it matters to:\\n\\n- use Git;\\n\\n- organise a high-quality code review;\\n\\n- synchronise the work through pull requests.\\n\\n3. The automation of the processes\\nThe team uses CI/CD in order to:\\n\\n- run the tests automatically;\\n\\n- build the artefacts;\\n\\n- deliver the product;\\n\\n- reduce the number of errors.\\n\\nThe main Agile methodologies\\nScrum\\nUses fixed iterations (sprints).\\nScrum has:\\n\\n- a Product Owner\\n\\n- a Scrum Master\\n\\n- the development team\\n\\nThe typical ceremonies:\\n\\n- Sprint Planning\\n\\n- the Daily Stand-up\\n\\n- the Sprint Review\\n\\n- the Sprint Retrospective\\n\\n- Backlog Refinement\\n\\nKanban\\nBased on the visualisation of the flow of tasks.\\nUses a board with columns (“To Do → In Progress → Done”).\\nThe main value is managing the flow and reducing the WIP (Work In Progress).\\nXP (Extreme Programming)\\nA focus on quality:\\n\\n- pair programming;\\n\\n- TDD;\\n\\n- continuous integration;\\n\\n- refactoring as part of the process.\\n\\nThe key patterns and practices\\n\\n- The backlog — the list of tasks, requirements and improvements.\\n\\n- User stories — the formalisation of the requirements from the user\'s point of view.\\n\\n- The Definition of Done — the criteria for a task being ready.\\n\\n- Velocity — how much the team manages in an iteration.\\n\\n- Planning Poker — the collective estimation of tasks.\\n\\n- The retrospective — an analysis of the process and of the improvements.\\n\\n- The pull request flow — the process of changing code.\\n\\n- Git-flow — the rules of branching.\\n\\nConclusion\\nAgile is not a set of rituals but a philosophy of flexible development. Teams working by Agile deliver value faster, improve their interaction and react to changes faster, thanks to iterativeness, transparency and the automation of the processes.",null],[3072,"In Agile, tasks usually pass through the stages: Backlog, Ready, In Progress, In Review, In Testing, Done. Every stage denotes a phase of the task\'s life cycle: from the idea to fully finished functionality. Scrum uses similar statuses within a sprint. Kanban relies on the flow: a task moves from right to left until it is completed. These statuses help manage the work, keep the team\'s load under control and ensure the transparency of the processes.","In Agile it matters to visualise the flow of tasks, so that the team understands what is being done, what is ready to be worked on and what is blocked. Different teams use different sets of statuses, but there are several generally accepted ones.\\nThe main statuses of tasks in Agile\\n1. Backlog\\nAll of the tasks, ideas and requirements the team has not yet taken into work.\\n2. Ready (To Do)\\nThe task is ready to be carried out, has been clarified and prioritised.\\n3. In Progress\\nA developer is working on the task.\\nAt this stage it matters to:\\n\\n- make small commits;\\n\\n- push the changes to the branch;\\n\\n- pass CI regularly.\\n\\n4. In Review\\nThe task is waiting for a code review.\\nAt this stage the quality rises and the number of errors in production falls.\\n5. In Testing\\nThe testers check the task, or CI runs the automatic tests.\\n6. Done\\nThe functionality is ready: tested, deployed, accepted by the user or by the PO.\\nAdditional statuses\\n\\n- Blocked — the task cannot be carried out because of a dependency.\\n\\n- On Hold — the work has been temporarily suspended.\\n\\n- Ready for Release — the task has passed QA and is sitting in the release list.\\n\\nScrum-specific states\\nIn Scrum a task is considered “Done” only if the Definition of Done has been met:\\n\\n- the tests have been written;\\n\\n- it has passed the code review;\\n\\n- the documentation has been done;\\n\\n- it has been deployed to the test environment.\\n\\nKanban-specific states\\nKanban is oriented towards WIP (Work in Progress) limits, so the statuses are usually:\\n\\nConclusion\\nWorking statuses in Agile let you track the movement of tasks transparently, reveal the bottlenecks of the process and synchronise the team\'s work. The correct visualisation of the statuses is one of the key tools of modern development.","Backlog → Selected → In Progress → Review → Done"],[3073,"RPS is the number of requests per second a system can process. It helps you understand a service\'s throughput. RPS is assessed by means of load testing, gradually increasing the number of requests and watching the response time and the number of errors. That lets you determine the limits of the performance and the bottlenecks.","The definition of RPS\\nRPS (Requests Per Second) is a metric showing the number of requests a system is capable of processing in one second.\\nIt is used to measure the performance of web applications and server services.\\nHow RPS is assessed\\n\\n- Load testing\\n\\n- The tools applied: `wrk`, `ab`, `k6`, `JMeter`, `Locust`.\\n\\n- A gradual increase of the load helps you find the point of degradation.\\n\\n- The analysis of the latency\\n\\n- The average, P95 and P99 are measured.\\n\\n- The growth of the latency as the load increases is watched.\\n\\n- The assessment of the errors\\n\\n- `5xx` statuses, timeouts and connection failures are recorded.\\n\\n- When there start to be too many errors, the RPS is considered to have been reached.\\n\\n- The monitoring of the resources\\n\\n- the CPU\\n\\n- memory\\n\\n- the disk\\n\\n- the network\\nThe bottleneck determines the ceiling of the RPS.\\n\\nAn example of a tool\\n\\nConclusion\\nRPS reflects a service\'s throughput and is assessed by load testing with an analysis of the latency and the errors.","wrk -t4 -c100 -d30s http://localhost:8000"],[3074,"An SLA is an agreed level of service quality — the percentage of the time it is available, for example. It helps formalise the obligations between teams or service providers. An SLA lets you assess the stability of a system, take decisions on redundancy and tune the monitoring processes.","The definition of an SLA\\nAn SLA (Service Level Agreement) is a document or a metric that fixes the level of availability and quality of a service that a team or a provider undertakes to maintain.\\nIt sets specific guarantees, for example: “the service is available 99.9% of the time”.\\nThe main parameters of an SLA\\n\\n- Availability\\n\\n- Stated as a percentage, 99.95% for example.\\n\\n- Response Time\\n\\n- The required P95 and P99 values.\\n\\n- The permissible level of errors\\n\\n- The maximum percentage of failures or errors.\\n\\n- The recovery time (MTTR)\\n\\n- The maximum permissible time for eliminating an incident.\\n\\nWhat an SLA is for\\n\\n- Defines the minimum quality of the service.\\n\\n- Establishes expectations between teams.\\n\\n- Helps assess the work of the operations team.\\n\\n- Determines the resources needed (redundancy, replication, monitoring).\\n\\nAn example of the wording\\n«The API must be available 99.9% of the time per month; the response time to a request does not exceed 200 ms for P95».\\nConclusion\\nAn SLA helps formalise the requirements for a service\'s stability and manage the quality of its operation.",null],[3075,"Profiling lets you measure which parts of a program consume the most time or resources. Server applications are profiled by means of tracing, sampling or built-in profilers. The results help you find problem functions, slow queries or leaks. Decisions on optimisation are taken on the basis of profiling.","What profiling is\\nProfiling is the process of measuring a program\'s performance with the aim of finding the bottlenecks.\\nIt lets you understand where the CPU, the memory and the waiting time are spent.\\nThe main approaches\\n\\n- Sampling\\n\\n- The profiler periodically reads the state of the stack.\\n\\n- A small effect on performance.\\n\\n- Used in the tools: `py-spy`, `perf`.\\n\\n- Tracing\\n\\n- Every event (a function call) is recorded.\\n\\n- More precise, but heavy for the system.\\n\\n- Tools: `cProfile`, `yappi`.\\n\\n- Memory profiling\\n\\n- Reveals leaks and the frequent allocation of objects.\\n\\n- Tools: `memory_profiler`, `tracemalloc`.\\n\\n- Profiling in production\\n\\n- Thin, low-overhead profilers.\\n\\n- Flamegraphs for the analysis.\\n\\nAn example of Python profiling\\n\\nWhat you look for when profiling\\n\\n- hot spots\\n\\n- functions with a high CPU time\\n\\n- blocking operations\\n\\n- inefficient data structures\\n\\n- memory leaks\\n\\nConclusion\\nProfiling helps you understand a service\'s real performance and choose the places to optimise.","import cProfile\\n\\ndef heavy():\\n    for _ in range(10**6):\\n        pass\\n\\ncProfile.run(\\"heavy()\\")"],[3076,"Apache Airflow is a tool for the orchestration of tasks and workflows. It lets you describe chains of tasks in the form of code and manage their execution. Airflow is often used for ETL pipelines, analytical calculations and periodic tasks. It does not do the work itself but manages its starting and its order. Airflow\'s main advantage is the transparency and manageability of the processes.","Airflow appeared as an answer to the problem of managing complex chains of tasks that have to be carried out in a particular order and on a schedule.\\nThe definition\\nApache Airflow is a platform for the orchestration of workflows, in which the tasks and the dependencies between them are described in the form of Python code.\\n1. Which problems Airflow solves\\nAirflow is not about business logic but about the management of processes.\\nThe typical scenarios:\\n\\n- ETL and ELT pipelines;\\n\\n- loading data into warehouses;\\n\\n- periodic calculations;\\n\\n- the automation of batch tasks.\\n\\n2. What Airflow does and what it does not\\nIt matters to understand the boundaries clearly.\\nAirflow:\\n\\n- starts tasks;\\n\\n- watches the order of execution;\\n\\n- retries steps that have failed;\\n\\n- gives you monitoring and a UI.\\n\\nAirflow does not:\\n\\n- replace Spark, Python scripts or SQL;\\n\\n- it is not intended for real-time processing;\\n\\n- it is not a message broker.\\n\\n3. Why workflows are described in code\\nAirflow uses Python, because:\\n\\n- the dependencies can be described explicitly;\\n\\n- the code is easy to version;\\n\\n- complex logic is simpler to maintain.\\n\\nAn example of the idea (simplified):\\n\\n4. Where Airflow is used most often\\nIn practice Airflow often stands:\\n\\n- between Kafka and the warehouses;\\n\\n- in front of the analytical data marts;\\n\\n- in a data platform.\\n\\nIt manages when and in what order the steps are carried out, but not how exactly they compute the data.\\n5. A brief conclusion\\nApache Airflow is a task orchestrator, which is ideally suited to complex, dependent and repeating workflows. It is worth using when control, transparency and the reproducibility of the processes matter.","task_a >> task_b >> task_c"],[3077,"Airflow consists of several key components: the scheduler, the webserver, the metadata database and the executor. The scheduler decides which tasks to start and when. The webserver provides the interface for monitoring and management. The metadata database holds the state of the DAGs and the tasks. The executor is responsible for the actual execution of the tasks.","The Airflow architecture is a set of services that jointly manage the planning, starting and monitoring of workflows.\\n1. The scheduler\\nThe scheduler is Airflow\'s «brain».\\nIt is responsible for:\\n\\n- reading the DAG files;\\n\\n- determining which tasks are ready to be started;\\n\\n- putting the tasks into the queue for execution.\\n\\nImportant:\\n\\n- the scheduler does not carry out the tasks itself;\\n\\n- it only takes the decision as to what to start and when.\\n\\n2. The executor\\nThe executor determines where and how a task will be carried out.\\nThe typical options:\\n\\n- `SequentialExecutor` — locally, one task at a time (dev);\\n\\n- `LocalExecutor` — in parallel on one machine;\\n\\n- `CeleryExecutor` — distributed through a queue;\\n\\n- `KubernetesExecutor` — every task in a separate pod.\\n\\nThe choice of executor directly affects scalability.\\n3. Workers\\nWorkers are the processes or containers that:\\n\\n- receive a task from the executor;\\n\\n- carry out the user\'s code;\\n\\n- return the result of the execution.\\n\\nThis is exactly where the Python scripts, the SQL queries and the API calls are carried out.\\n4. The metadata database\\nThe metadata DB holds the state of the system:\\n\\n- the statuses of the DAGs and the tasks;\\n\\n- the schedules;\\n\\n- retries;\\n\\n- XCom;\\n\\n- information about the runs.\\n\\nMost often PostgreSQL or MySQL is used.\\nImportant:\\n\\n- the loss of the database = the loss of Airflow\'s state;\\n\\n- it is a critical component.\\n\\n5. The webserver\\nThe webserver provides the UI:\\n\\n- viewing the DAGs;\\n\\n- the tasks\' logs;\\n\\n- a manual start;\\n\\n- the management of pauses and of parameters.\\n\\nThe webserver:\\n\\n- takes no part in the execution of the tasks;\\n\\n- can be scaled independently.\\n\\n6. A brief conclusion\\nAirflow consists of a set of specialised components, where the scheduler takes the decisions, the executor and the workers carry out the tasks, and the metadata database holds the state. An understanding of this scheme matters for stable operation and for scaling.",null],[3078,"A DAG in Airflow is a description of a workflow in the form of a directed acyclic graph. It defines the tasks and the dependencies between them. A DAG is described in Python code. Airflow uses the DAG in order to understand the order in which the tasks are to be carried out. Cycles in a DAG are forbidden.","A DAG is the central concept of Airflow. Without it Airflow does not know which tasks exist and in what order to start them.\\nThe definition\\nA DAG (Directed Acyclic Graph) is a directed graph without cycles, which describes tasks and the dependencies between them.\\n1. What a DAG consists of\\nA DAG includes:\\n\\n- a unique name;\\n\\n- the schedule on which it starts;\\n\\n- a set of tasks;\\n\\n- the dependencies between the tasks.\\n\\nAn example of the idea of a DAG:\\n\\n2. Why a DAG is acyclic\\nCycles are forbidden, because:\\n\\n- it would be impossible to determine the start of the execution;\\n\\n- the tasks would depend on one another endlessly.\\n\\nAirflow checks the DAG and will not start it if there is a cycle.\\n3. A DAG as a description, not an execution\\nIt matters to understand:\\n\\n- a DAG is a description of a workflow;\\n\\n- the tasks are not carried out when the DAG is declared;\\n\\n- the execution happens later, on a schedule or manually.\\n\\nThat is a frequent beginners\' mistake.\\n4. The planning of the runs\\nEvery run of a DAG:\\n\\n- is called a DAG Run;\\n\\n- has a logical date;\\n\\n- contains a set of Task Instances.\\n\\nThat lets you:\\n\\n- replay past runs;\\n\\n- analyse the history of the executions.\\n\\n5. Practical recommendations\\nWhen designing a DAG:\\n\\n- make the tasks small and independent;\\n\\n- avoid complex logic at the level of the DAG file;\\n\\n- describe the dependencies clearly.\\n\\n6. A brief conclusion\\nA DAG is a declarative description of a workflow in Airflow. It sets the structure and the order in which the tasks are carried out, but it is not responsible for their implementation.","extract >> transform >> load"],[3079,"An operator in Airflow is a template for carrying out a specific action. It describes what exactly needs to be done: run Python code, execute SQL or call an API. A task is created on the basis of an operator inside a DAG. One operator can be used many times. An operator does not manage the order of execution — the DAG takes care of that.","Airflow separates the concepts of «what to do» and «when to do it». The operator is responsible for the first.\\nThe definition\\nAn operator is an object in Airflow that describes a single action carried out within a DAG.\\n1. An operator and a task are not the same thing\\nIt matters to distinguish:\\n\\n- an operator is a class (a template of behaviour);\\n\\n- a task is an instance of an operator inside a DAG.\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nHere:\\n\\n- `PythonOperator` is the operator;\\n\\n- `process_data` is the task.\\n\\n2. What an operator is responsible for\\nAn operator determines:\\n\\n- which code is carried out;\\n\\n- which parameters are passed;\\n\\n- how to handle success or an error.\\n\\nBut it does not determine:\\n\\n- the schedule;\\n\\n- the dependencies;\\n\\n- the retries at the DAG level (those are set separately).\\n\\n3. Where an operator\'s code is carried out\\nAn operator\'s code:\\n\\n- is carried out on a worker;\\n\\n- is started by the executor;\\n\\n- is isolated from the scheduler.\\n\\nThat matters for an understanding of the resources and the failures.\\n4. The reuse of operators\\nOne and the same operator:\\n\\n- can be used in different DAGs;\\n\\n- many tasks can be created on its basis;\\n\\n- it is easy to parameterise.\\n\\nThat makes Airflow flexible and extensible.\\n5. Practical recommendations\\n\\n- use ready-made operators if they exist;\\n\\n- do not overload an operator with business logic;\\n\\n- move complex logic out into separate modules.\\n\\n6. A brief conclusion\\nAn operator is the building block of tasks in Airflow. It describes a specific action, while the DAG determines when and in what order those actions are carried out.","PythonOperator(\\n    task_id=\\"process_data\\",\\n    python_callable=process\\n)"],[3080,"In Airflow there are operators for executing code, working with databases, interacting with external services and managing dependencies. The most basic ones are PythonOperator, BashOperator and the SQL operators. There are operators for cloud services and for Kubernetes. There are also special operators for managing a workflow. Usually you choose the simplest operator that solves the task.","Airflow provides a large number of operators, but they all fit into a few logical categories. An understanding of these categories makes designing a DAG simpler.\\nThe definition\\nAirflow operator types are groups of operators united by their purpose and by the way they carry out tasks.\\n1. The basic operators\\nThese are general-purpose operators, which are used most often.\\n1.1. PythonOperator\\nUsed to carry out Python functions.\\n\\n- suits light logic;\\n\\n- often acts as the «glue» between the steps.\\n\\n1.2. BashOperator\\nCarries out shell commands.\\n\\n- useful for scripts;\\n\\n- often applied for utilities and a CLI.\\n\\n2. Operators for working with databases\\nThese operators encapsulate work with SQL and with warehouses.\\nExamples:\\n\\n- `PostgresOperator`;\\n\\n- `MySqlOperator`;\\n\\n- `ClickHouseOperator` (through the providers).\\n\\nThey:\\n\\n- execute SQL queries;\\n\\n- manage the transactions;\\n\\n- make connecting simpler.\\n\\n3. Transfer operators\\nUsed to transfer data between systems.\\nExamples of the tasks:\\n\\n- copying data from a database into a warehouse;\\n\\n- uploading files;\\n\\n- moving data between services.\\n\\nThat is convenient for ETL scenarios.\\n4. Cloud and Kubernetes operators\\nAirflow has integrations with the clouds.\\nExamples:\\n\\n- operators for S3, GCS;\\n\\n- `KubernetesPodOperator`.\\n\\nThey let you:\\n\\n- run tasks in containers;\\n\\n- scale the load;\\n\\n- isolate the environment.\\n\\n5. Service operators\\nResponsible for managing the workflow rather than for business logic.\\nExamples:\\n\\n- `DummyOperator`;\\n\\n- `BranchPythonOperator`;\\n\\n- `ShortCircuitOperator`.\\n\\nThey help you:\\n\\n- build conditional logic;\\n\\n- manage the branching of the DAG.\\n\\n6. A brief conclusion\\nAirflow\'s operators divide into basic, integration and service ones. In practice PythonOperator and the database operators are used most often, and the rest are brought in as the need arises.",null],[3081,"Sensors in Airflow are special operators that wait for a particular condition to arrive. They can wait for a file, a record in a database or the completion of an external task. A sensor periodically checks the condition until it is met or the timeout expires. After that the DAG continues its execution. Sensors are often used to synchronise pipelines.","In many workflows what matters is not simply starting a task on a schedule but waiting until an external resource becomes available. That is exactly what sensors exist for in Airflow.\\nThe definition\\nA sensor is an Airflow operator that blocks the execution of a DAG until a given condition is met.\\n1. What sensors do exactly\\nSensors:\\n\\n- regularly check the state of an external resource;\\n\\n- «sleep» between the checks;\\n\\n- finish successfully when the condition has been met.\\n\\nThe typical conditions:\\n\\n- a file has appeared in S3;\\n\\n- a row has appeared in a table;\\n\\n- another DAG has finished.\\n\\n2. Examples of sensors\\nAirflow provides many ready-made sensors:\\n\\n- `FileSensor`;\\n\\n- `ExternalTaskSensor`;\\n\\n- `S3KeySensor`;\\n\\n- `SqlSensor`.\\n\\nEach of them encapsulates the logic of the check.\\n3. A sensor is not a handler\\nIt matters to remember:\\n\\n- a sensor processes nothing;\\n\\n- it only waits;\\n\\n- the business logic should be in other tasks.\\n\\nThat is a frequent beginners\' mistake.\\n4. The modes in which sensors work\\nSensors can work:\\n\\n- in active-waiting mode;\\n\\n- in asynchronous (deferrable) mode.\\n\\nThe choice of mode affects the use of resources.\\n5. A brief conclusion\\nSensors let Airflow synchronise with the outside world and manage dependencies between systems. They need to be used deliberately, since they can strongly affect the resources.",null],[3082,"It makes sense to use sensors when you have to wait for an external event before continuing the workflow. That can be the appearance of data, the completion of another DAG or the readiness of an external service. They are useful for synchronising pipelines. Sensors do not suit a long wait without a pressing need. In such cases it is better to look for event-based or asynchronous alternatives.","Sensors are a powerful tool, but when used incorrectly they can become a source of problems with performance and resources.\\nThe definition\\nA sensor usage pattern is a scenario in which waiting for an external condition is architecturally justified.\\n1. The correct scenarios of use\\nSensors suit well when:\\n1.1. There is a hard external dependency\\nFor example:\\n\\n- the data is loaded by an outside system;\\n\\n- starting the next step without it is impossible.\\n\\n1.2. Pipelines need to be synchronised\\nA frequent case:\\n\\n- one DAG prepares the data;\\n\\n- the second DAG waits for the first to finish.\\n\\nFor that an `ExternalTaskSensor` is often used.\\n2. Borderline and bad scenarios\\nSensors suit badly if:\\n2.1. The waiting is very long\\n\\n- hours or days of waiting;\\n\\n- a large number of sensors «hanging» at the same time.\\n\\nThat creates a load on the system.\\n2.2. The condition can be made event-based\\nIf the external service can:\\n\\n- send an event;\\n\\n- call an API;\\n\\n- put a message into a queue.\\n\\nIn such cases it is better to:\\n\\n- start the DAG on an event;\\n\\n- use Kafka or a webhook.\\n\\n3. Practical recommendations\\n\\n- always set a reasonable `timeout`;\\n\\n- increase the `poke_interval` for infrequent checks;\\n\\n- use deferrable sensors when the wait is long.\\n\\n4. A brief conclusion\\nSensors are worth using when the workflow cannot continue without waiting. For long waits and mass dependencies it is better to choose asynchronous or event-based solutions.",null],[3083,"Polling is the periodic checking of a condition at a given time interval. The sensor «wakes up», checks the condition and, if it has not been met, falls asleep again. The interval is set by the poke_interval parameter. Such an approach is simple, but it can consume resources if there are many sensors. Polling therefore needs to be used carefully.","Polling is the basic mechanism by which the classic sensors in Airflow work. It is simple to implement, but it has its limitations.\\nThe definition\\nPolling is the regular checking of the state of an external resource at fixed time intervals.\\n1. A sensor\'s life cycle\\nA sensor\'s work looks like this:\\n\\n- The sensor starts.\\n\\n- It checks the condition (poke).\\n\\n- If the condition is false — it waits for the `poke_interval`.\\n\\n- It repeats the steps until it succeeds or times out.\\n\\nAn example of the logic:\\n\\n2. The key parameters of polling\\nThe main settings of a sensor:\\n2.1. `poke_interval`\\n\\n- the interval between the checks;\\n\\n- too small → a high load;\\n\\n- too large → a high latency.\\n\\n2.2. `timeout`\\n\\n- the maximum waiting time;\\n\\n- once it expires the sensor fails with an error.\\n\\n3. The modes of execution\\nThe classic sensors:\\n\\n- occupy a worker for the whole time of the waiting;\\n\\n- block a slot of the executor.\\n\\nThat is the main source of problems when scaling.\\n4. Why polling is expensive\\nIf:\\n\\n- there are many DAGs;\\n\\n- there are many sensors;\\n\\n- the timeouts are long.\\n\\nThen:\\n\\n- the workers stand idle;\\n\\n- the tasks wait their turn;\\n\\n- the overall throughput falls.\\n\\n5. A brief conclusion\\nPolling is a simple and understandable waiting mechanism, but it scales badly. It is worth using only for short and infrequent waits, or replacing with deferrable sensors.","while not condition():\\n    sleep(poke_interval)"],[3084,"When used incorrectly, sensors can occupy workers for a long time. That leads to a shortage of slots for real tasks. The scheduler starts to work more slowly and the DAGs queue up. The load on external systems also grows because of the frequent checks. Airflow ends up unstable.","Sensors seem harmless, because they «do nothing», but in practice they often become the main source of problems with resources.\\nThe definition\\nA sensor resource issue is the situation in which waiting for conditions in Airflow leads to computing resources being blocked.\\n1. The blocking of workers\\nThe classic sensors:\\n\\n- occupy a worker for the whole time of the waiting;\\n\\n- do not free up the executor\'s slot.\\n\\nThe consequences:\\n\\n- the workers stand idle;\\n\\n- useful tasks cannot start;\\n\\n- the execution queue grows.\\n\\n2. The overloading of the scheduler\\nWith a large number of sensors:\\n\\n- the scheduler constantly checks their state;\\n\\n- the time it takes to process a DAG increases;\\n\\n- the latency of planning the tasks rises.\\n\\nThat is especially noticeable in large Airflow installations.\\n3. An excessive load on external systems\\nFrequent polling can:\\n\\n- create unnecessary requests to a database or an API;\\n\\n- lead to a rate limit;\\n\\n- worsen the stability of the external services.\\n\\nSometimes the problem is not in Airflow but in the system it «pokes».\\n4. An increase in the DAG\'s execution time\\nEven if the DAG is simple:\\n\\n- the sensors can hang for hours;\\n\\n- the whole DAG is considered «in progress»;\\n\\n- it is harder to analyse the real execution time.\\n\\n5. Difficulties with scaling\\nThe more sensors there are:\\n\\n- the more workers are needed;\\n\\n- the higher the cost of the infrastructure;\\n\\n- the harder it is to predict the system\'s behaviour.\\n\\n6. Practical recommendations\\nTo avoid problems:\\n\\n- do not use sensors for a long wait;\\n\\n- increase the `poke_interval`;\\n\\n- always set a `timeout`;\\n\\n- move over to deferrable sensors where possible.\\n\\n7. A brief conclusion\\nThe incorrect use of sensors leads to resources being blocked, to Airflow working more slowly and to external systems being overloaded. Sensors call for careful and deliberate application.",null],[3085,"Ordinary sensors constantly occupy a worker while they wait. Deferrable sensors free the worker up and «fall asleep» until the event arrives. They use an asynchronous model of waiting. That lets you substantially reduce the load on the system. Deferrable sensors suit long waits better.","Deferrable sensors appeared as the solution to the problem of resources being blocked during a long wait for external conditions.\\nThe definition\\nA deferrable sensor is a sensor that uses an asynchronous waiting mechanism and does not block a worker.\\n1. Ordinary (blocking) sensors\\nA classic sensor:\\n\\n- starts on a worker;\\n\\n- carries out polling;\\n\\n- «sleeps» between the checks;\\n\\n- holds on to the executor\'s slot.\\n\\nThe problem:\\n\\n- the worker is occupied although there is no useful work.\\n\\n2. Deferrable sensors\\nA deferrable sensor:\\n\\n- carries out the first check;\\n\\n- if the condition has not been met, it defers;\\n\\n- frees the worker up;\\n\\n- resumes when the event arrives or on a timer.\\n\\nFor that it uses:\\n\\n- the triggerer process;\\n\\n- asynchronous triggers.\\n\\n3. The triggerer and events\\nThe triggerer:\\n\\n- is a separate Airflow component;\\n\\n- tracks events asynchronously;\\n\\n- «wakes» the task when the condition has been met.\\n\\nThat lets you:\\n\\n- hold thousands of waiting sensors;\\n\\n- avoid increasing the number of workers.\\n\\n4. When to use deferrable sensors\\nThey are especially useful:\\n\\n- when waiting for external APIs;\\n\\n- with long timeouts;\\n\\n- in large Airflow installations.\\n\\nThe limitation:\\n\\n- not every sensor has a deferrable version;\\n\\n- they are harder to debug.\\n\\n5. Practical recommendations\\n\\n- use deferrable sensors for waits of > a few minutes;\\n\\n- check whether the operator supports deferrable mode;\\n\\n- keep an eye on the triggerer\'s load.\\n\\n6. A brief conclusion\\nDeferrable sensors solve the key problem of the classic sensors — the blocking of resources. For modern production systems they are the preferred option when conditions are waited on for a long time.",null],[3086,"Your own sensor is created by inheriting from BaseSensorOperator. In it you implement the poke method, which checks the condition. Airflow calls this method at the given interval. For an asynchronous version the deferrable model and triggers are used. Such an approach lets you implement waiting for any external conditions.","Sometimes the standard sensors are not enough, and then you have to write your own. Airflow provides an understandable and extensible mechanism for that.\\nThe definition\\nA custom sensor is a user\'s own sensor, implemented through inheritance from an Airflow base class.\\n1. The classic (blocking) sensor\\nThe simplest option is a synchronous sensor.\\n1.1. The basic structure\\nYou have to:\\n\\n- inherit from `BaseSensorOperator`;\\n\\n- implement the `poke` method.\\n\\nAn example of a simplified implementation:\\n\\nIf `poke` has returned:\\n\\n- `True` → the sensor has finished;\\n\\n- `False` → Airflow will wait the `poke_interval` and try again.\\n\\n2. The deferrable sensor\\nFor long waits it is better to use the asynchronous model.\\n2.1. The main idea\\nA deferrable sensor:\\n\\n- checks the condition;\\n\\n- if it is not ready — calls `defer`;\\n\\n- hands control over to the trigger.\\n\\n2.2. The simplified logic\\n\\nThe trigger:\\n\\n- waits for the event asynchronously;\\n\\n- returns control to the sensor.\\n\\n3. When to write your own sensor\\nIt makes sense if:\\n\\n- there is no ready-made sensor;\\n\\n- the logic of the check is specific;\\n\\n- it matters to optimise the resources.\\n\\n4. Practical recommendations\\n\\n- always set a `timeout`;\\n\\n- log the checks;\\n\\n- test the sensor separately from the DAG.\\n\\n5. A brief conclusion\\nYour own sensors let you adapt Airflow to any external conditions. For short waits blocking sensors will do; for long ones, the deferrable model with triggers.","from airflow.sensors.base import BaseSensorOperator\\n\\nclass MySensor(BaseSensorOperator):\\n    def poke(self, context):\\n        # check the condition\\n        return check_condition()\\n\\ndef execute(self, context):\\n    if not condition():\\n        self.defer(trigger=MyTrigger())"],[3087,"In Airflow tasks do not pass data to one another directly. A special mechanism, XCom, is used for that. One task can save the data and another read it. Such an approach lets you preserve the isolation of the tasks. XCom is intended only for small volumes of data, however.","Airflow is built so that the tasks are as independent as possible. The passing of data between them is therefore implemented not directly but through a centralised mechanism.\\nThe definition\\nInter-task communication is the way of passing data between Airflow tasks without direct calls between them.\\n1. Why data cannot be passed directly\\nAirflow\'s tasks:\\n\\n- can be carried out on different workers;\\n\\n- can be started at different times;\\n\\n- do not share common memory.\\n\\nTherefore:\\n\\n- you cannot simply return a value from one task into another;\\n\\n- you cannot use global variables.\\n\\n2. The main mechanism — XCom\\nTo pass data, Airflow uses XCom:\\n\\n- the data is saved in the metadata database;\\n\\n- it is tied to a specific task and DAG run;\\n\\n- it can be read by other tasks.\\n\\nAn example of the idea:\\n\\n3. Alternative approaches\\nFor real data the following are used more often:\\n\\n- databases;\\n\\n- object stores (S3, GCS);\\n\\n- files;\\n\\n- external services.\\n\\nAirflow manages the process but does not become the transport for the data.\\n4. Practical recommendations\\n\\n- pass only metadata through XCom;\\n\\n- keep large data in external systems;\\n\\n- document explicitly which XComs are used.\\n\\n5. A brief conclusion\\nIn Airflow data is passed between tasks through XCom or external stores. XCom is convenient for small values, but it is not intended for transferring data.","context[\\"ti\\"].xcom_push(key=\\"result\\", value=42)"],[3088,"XCom is the mechanism for passing small amounts of data between Airflow tasks. It lets one task save a value and another read it. XCom is held in the metadata database. It is usually used to pass identifiers, flags or paths to data. XCom does not suit large data.","XCom (cross-communication) is the standard way of exchanging data between tasks within one DAG Run.\\nThe definition\\nXCom is Airflow\'s mechanism for passing small values between tasks through the metadata database.\\n1. How XCom works\\nThe process looks like this:\\n\\n- The task is carried out.\\n\\n- It saves a value through `xcom_push`.\\n\\n- Another task reads it through `xcom_pull`.\\n\\nImportant:\\n\\n- an XCom is tied to a `task_id` and a `dag_run`;\\n\\n- the data is serialised and saved in the database.\\n\\n2. Automatic XCom\\nSome operators:\\n\\n- put the result into XCom automatically;\\n\\n- PythonOperator, for example, can return a value.\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nThe returned value will become an XCom.\\n3. The typical scenarios of use\\nXCom suits well for:\\n\\n- passing record IDs;\\n\\n- passing dates and flags;\\n\\n- passing paths to files;\\n\\n- passing small configurations.\\n\\n4. Where XCom is held\\nBy default:\\n\\n- XCom is held in the metadata database;\\n\\n- it takes part in the backups and the migrations.\\n\\nThat makes it reliable, but not fast for large data.\\n5. Practical recommendations\\n\\n- use short, understandable keys;\\n\\n- do not abuse XCom;\\n\\n- clean up old DAG Runs where necessary.\\n\\n6. A brief conclusion\\nXCom is a convenient mechanism for passing small amounts of data and metadata between tasks. It makes building a workflow simpler, but it does not replace full data stores.","def calculate():\\n    return 10"],[3089,"Webpack is a tool for bundling JavaScript modules and other assets such as CSS or images. It lets you combine files into one or several output bundles, optimise them for use in the browser and manage dependencies.","Webpack is a popular module bundler that lets developers manage files and dependencies in a project efficiently. Its main job is to turn code into a browser-friendly format. Webpack uses the idea of a dependency graph: each entry point represents a main file, and every module it imports is analysed and included in the resulting bundle.\\nFor example, for a project with the files `index.js` and `style.css`, Webpack combines them into a single file:\\n\\nIn modern web development Webpack is often used together with plugins and loaders in order to process various file formats (for example transpiling JavaScript through Babel or minifying CSS).","module.exports = {\\n    entry: \'./src/index.js\', // Entry point\\n    output: {\\n        filename: \'bundle.js\', // Name of the resulting file\\n        path: __dirname + \'/dist\', // Folder for the output files\\n    },\\n};"],[3090,"Entry points are the files from which Webpack starts building the dependency graph. They are set in the configuration through the `entry` property. You can specify a single entry point for small applications, or several for more complex ones.","In Webpack an entry point (`entry`) is the initial file at which the analysis and bundling of dependencies begins. Usually this is the application\'s main JavaScript file, for example `index.js`. Once an entry point is given, Webpack finds every related module in order to produce one or several output files.\\nAn example configuration:\\n\\nIf your application is complex, you can specify several entry points:\\n\\nIn that case Webpack creates two separate bundles, for example `app.bundle.js` and `admin.bundle.js`. This is useful for code splitting, in order to optimise loading in the browser.","module.exports = {\\n    entry: \'./src/index.js\', // A single entry point\\n};\\n\\nmodule.exports = {\\n    entry: {\\n        app: \'./src/app.js\',\\n        admin: \'./src/admin.js\',\\n    },\\n};"],[3091,"Webpack\'s output property (`output`) states where and in what format files are saved after the build. It sets the path to the folder and the name of the resulting file.","The `output` property in Webpack is responsible for where the bundled files are saved. It defines the path to the output folder and the naming template for the files. For example:\\n\\nWebpack also supports dynamic file naming through templates:\\n\\nThis is especially useful for optimisation: hashing lets browsers download only the files that changed, reducing the amount of data transferred.\\nIt is important to understand that `output` works together with other settings, such as entry points, plugins and loaders.","module.exports = {\\n    output: {\\n        filename: \'bundle.js\', // Name of the output file\\n        path: __dirname + \'/dist\', // Path to the folder\\n    },\\n};\\n\\nmodule.exports = {\\n    output: {\\n        filename: \'[name].[contenthash].js\', // Dynamic name (for caching)\\n        path: __dirname + \'/dist\',\\n    },\\n};"],[3092,"Loaders in Webpack are tools that let it process files of different formats (CSS, images, TypeScript and others) before they are added to the bundle. They transform the source code so that Webpack can use it.","Webpack works with JavaScript, but thanks to loaders it can also process other kinds of files, for example CSS, images, fonts or TypeScript. Loaders are described in the Webpack configuration in the `module.rules` section. They are applied to the files that match particular conditions.\\nAn example of using a loader for CSS:\\n\\nHere `css-loader` processes the CSS files and `style-loader` inserts the styles into the DOM. Loaders are transformation chains that turn source files into a format ready for bundling.","module.exports = {\\n    module: {\\n        rules: [\\n            {\\n              test: /\\\\.css$/, // Applied to .css files\\n              use: [\'style-loader\', \'css-loader\'], // css-loader runs first, then style-loader\\n            },\\n        ],\\n    },\\n};"],[3093,"Plugins in Webpack are tools for extending what the build can do. They carry out tasks such as minifying code, generating HTML files, caching and others.","Plugins give deeper control over the build process than loaders do. They work with the stages of the build, from analysing modules to generating the final files. For example, the `HtmlWebpackPlugin` plugin automatically creates an HTML file and links the bundled scripts into it.\\nAn example of using a plugin:\\n\\nPlugins are registered through the `plugins` array in the configuration. They add functionality that cannot be implemented with loaders.","const HtmlWebpackPlugin = require(\'html-webpack-plugin\');\\n\\nmodule.exports = {\\n    plugins: [\\n        new HtmlWebpackPlugin({\\n            template: \'./src/index.html\', // Template for the HTML\\n        }),\\n    ],\\n};"],[3094,"Loaders transform files (for example TypeScript into JavaScript, or SCSS into CSS) so that Webpack can process them. Plugins, by contrast, work at a higher level — they add functionality to the build process, for example optimisation or the creation of new files.","The main difference between loaders and plugins is that they do different jobs:\\nLoaders:\\nLoaders deal with transforming individual files. They are applied at the file-processing stage, using transformation chains. For example, to turn SCSS into CSS:\\n\\nPlugins:\\nPlugins add functionality at the build stage. They perform complex tasks such as minifying code, linking scripts automatically or caching. For example, `TerserPlugin` minifies JavaScript:\\n\\nSo loaders process content, while plugins change the build process itself. Both tools work together in order to produce the finished product.","module: {\\n    rules: [\\n      {\\n          test: /\\\\.scss$/,\\n          use: [\'style-loader\', \'css-loader\', \'sass-loader\'],\\n      },\\n    ],\\n}\\n\\nconst TerserPlugin = require(\'terser-webpack-plugin\');\\n\\nmodule.exports = {\\n    optimization: {\\n      minimize: true,\\n      minimizer: [new TerserPlugin()],\\n    },\\n};"],[3095,"Webpack Dev Server provides a local development server that refreshes the page in the browser automatically when the code changes. This speeds up development and testing.","Webpack Dev Server is a tool that starts a local server for developing your application. Its main advantages:\\nAutomatic page refresh (hot reloading):\\nWhen you change the code, the server rebuilds the project and refreshes the browser automatically. This removes the need to reload the page by hand.\\nProxying requests:\\nWebpack Dev Server can proxy API requests, which is useful when working with a back end.\\nLocal development without writing files to disk:\\nThe server works in memory, which speeds the process up, since files are not written to disk.\\nAn example of configuring Webpack Dev Server:\\n\\nIt is a powerful tool for fast development, especially in large projects.","module.exports = {\\n    devServer: {\\n        contentBase: \'./dist\', // Directory for static files\\n        port: 8080, // Server port\\n        hot: true, // Enable hot reloading\\n    },\\n};"],[3096,"Several entry points are defined with an object in the `entry` configuration. Each entry point represents an independent module of the application, and Webpack creates a separate output file for each of them.","Complex applications may have different parts that work independently, for example a user panel and an admin panel. Several entry points are used to bundle them.\\nAn example configuration:\\n\\nIn this case Webpack creates two files: `main.bundle.js` and `admin.bundle.js`. This helps to separate the application\'s logic and to optimise how resources load in the browser.","module.exports = {\\n    entry: {\\n        main: \'./src/main.js\', // Main entry point\\n        admin: \'./src/admin.js\', // Admin panel\\n    },\\n    output: {\\n        filename: \'[name].bundle.js\', // The file name matches the entry point name\\n        path: __dirname + \'/dist\',\\n    },\\n};"],[3097,"Webpack CLI is a command-line interface that lets you run Webpack, manage settings and build the project. Without it you cannot use Webpack from the terminal.","Webpack CLI (Command Line Interface) provides commands for working with Webpack. With it you can start a build, change settings, pass parameters and manage the development process. For example:\\nStarting a build:\\n\\nSpecifying the mode:\\n\\nSpecifying a configuration:\\n\\nWebpack CLI is the bridge between your configuration and Webpack itself. It simplifies interaction with Webpack and lets you test and debug a project faster.","npx webpack\\n\\nnpx webpack --mode production\\n\\nnpx webpack --config webpack.custom.config.js"],[3098,"Webpack supports dynamic imports through the `import()` function, letting modules be loaded only when they are needed. This helps split code into chunks and reduces the application\'s load time.","A dynamic import is a way of loading modules as they are needed. Instead of the traditional static import (`import ... from ...`), the `import()` function is used, which returns a promise. Webpack automatically splits such imports into separate chunks.\\nExample:\\n\\nWhen you press the button, Webpack loads the `module.js` file asynchronously. This lets you reduce the initial size of the JavaScript download and optimise the loading of heavy modules.","document.getElementById(\'load\').addEventListener(\'click\', () => {\\n    import(\'./module.js\')\\n        .then((module) => {\\n            module.default(); // Using the imported module\\n        })\\n        .catch((err) => console.error(\'Module loading error:\', err));\\n});"],[3099,"Webpack\'s mode (`mode`) determines exactly how the project is built. There are two main modes: `development` for development (with detailed sources) and `production` for optimised, minified code.","The mode (`mode`) in Webpack is responsible for the settings applied during the build. Specifying a mode simplifies the configuration by applying a set of default optimisations.\\nThe available modes:\\n`development`:\\nUsed for local development. It enables detailed source maps and makes debugging easier.\\n`production`:\\nEnables optimisations such as minification and tree shaking, in order to reduce the size and speed up the application.\\nExample:\\n\\nIf no mode is specified, Webpack emits a warning. Choosing the right mode considerably simplifies the configuration and avoids unnecessary work.","module.exports = {\\n    mode: \'development\', // Or \'production\'\\n};"],[3100,"`babel-loader` is a Webpack loader that uses Babel to convert modern JavaScript (ES6+) into an older format supported by most browsers.","Babel is a transpiler that converts modern JavaScript into code supported by older browser versions. `babel-loader` is the tool that integrates Babel with Webpack, letting the code be transpiled during the build.\\nAn example configuration:\\n\\nThanks to Babel you can use the latest JavaScript features, such as async/await, optional chaining and modules, without worrying about browser incompatibility. `babel-loader` makes that process convenient and automatic.","module.exports = {\\n    module: {\\n        rules: [\\n            {\\n              test: /\\\\.js$/, // Processes every file with the .js extension\\n              exclude: /node_modules/, // Excludes library files\\n              use: {\\n                  loader: \'babel-loader\',\\n                  options: {\\n                      presets: [\'@babel/preset-env\'], // Preset for transpiling ES6+\\n                  },\\n              },\\n            },\\n        ],\\n    },\\n};"]]');export{e as default};